19
00:00:39,450 --> 00:00:40,620
Yeah, I think he did.

20
00:00:40,620 --> 00:00:42,570
But anyways.

21
00:00:42,570 --> 00:00:43,710
So what's interesting is--

22
00:00:43,710 --> 00:00:50,738
So it's amusing to actually look
at what they wrote in there.

23
00:00:50,738 --> 00:00:52,530
I don't know if it was
a brochure, or what.

24
00:00:52,530 --> 00:00:56,370
But they wrote in
there their goals.

25
00:00:56,370 --> 00:00:58,140
The font is a bit small.

26
00:00:58,140 --> 00:01:01,080
So the study is to proceed on
the basis of the conjecture

27
00:01:01,080 --> 00:01:04,739
that every aspect of
learning or any other feature

28
00:01:04,739 --> 00:01:07,680
of intelligence can, in
principle, be so precisely

29
00:01:07,680 --> 00:01:10,920
described that a machine
can be made to simulate it.

30
00:01:10,920 --> 00:01:11,490
Fantastic.

31
00:01:11,490 --> 00:01:13,150
So single machine.

32
00:01:13,150 --> 00:01:16,020
You want to simulate all
of human intelligence.

33
00:01:16,020 --> 00:01:19,080
And a carefully selected
group of scientists--

34
00:01:19,080 --> 00:01:21,270
and we think that we
can make, actually,

35
00:01:21,270 --> 00:01:27,000
the paragraph right before the
second set of red underlines,

36
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:27,642
is--

37
00:01:27,642 --> 00:01:29,100
we think that a
significant advance

38
00:01:29,100 --> 00:01:31,710
can be made in one or
two of these problems

39
00:01:31,710 --> 00:01:36,180
if a carefully selected
group of scientists

40
00:01:36,180 --> 00:01:39,210
work together for a summer.

41
00:01:39,210 --> 00:01:41,490
I don't think they knew of
AI winters then, actually.

42
00:01:41,490 --> 00:01:42,890
They didn't know of it then.

43
00:01:42,890 --> 00:01:47,160
And the third thing I'm using
is-- but the major obstacle

44
00:01:47,160 --> 00:01:51,000
is not lack of machine
capacity, but our inability

45
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:54,102
to write programs taking full
advantage of what we have.

46
00:01:54,102 --> 00:01:55,560
So while the goals
were noble, it's

47
00:01:55,560 --> 00:01:57,870
surprising how wrong
you can be in--

48
00:01:57,870 --> 00:02:00,060
with some of the smartest
people in the room.

49
00:02:00,060 --> 00:02:03,510
So Selfridge, a
neural network OG

50
00:02:03,510 --> 00:02:05,160
that wrote the
original pandemoniums--

51
00:02:05,160 --> 00:02:06,702
I think he got
everything, basically,

52
00:02:06,702 --> 00:02:09,120
except problems with
black box optimization.

53
00:02:09,120 --> 00:02:11,700
Then Minsky.

54
00:02:11,700 --> 00:02:13,680
Of course, Shannon.

55
00:02:13,680 --> 00:02:16,110
Solomonoff-- I think
this is Solomonoff MDL.

56
00:02:16,110 --> 00:02:19,740
In many ways, you can argue
that's the underpinning

57
00:02:19,740 --> 00:02:24,420
of self-supervised
learning today.

58
00:02:24,420 --> 00:02:28,080
But it's really amazing
to see the first--

59
00:02:28,080 --> 00:02:29,670
at least I don't
know if we'll be

60
00:02:29,670 --> 00:02:34,348
able to characterize
or write down

61
00:02:34,348 --> 00:02:35,640
all the rules for intelligence.

62
00:02:35,640 --> 00:02:37,015
So you can imagine
the approaches

63
00:02:37,015 --> 00:02:39,780
they were taking were all
these rule-based systems.

64
00:02:39,780 --> 00:02:44,160
And they couldn't be more
wrong on machine capacity.

65
00:02:44,160 --> 00:02:49,590
Today's transformers are-- they
don't-- they're data centers.

66
00:02:49,590 --> 00:02:54,600
And I guess they needed a
really, really long summer

67
00:02:54,600 --> 00:02:55,350
to solve this one.

68
00:02:55,350 --> 00:02:58,080
But yeah.

69
00:02:58,080 --> 00:03:03,870
So it's 1955, so
about, yeah, 60 years--

70
00:03:03,870 --> 00:03:04,710
no, no.

71
00:03:04,710 --> 00:03:07,160
Getting close to 70 years.

72
00:03:07,160 --> 00:03:11,010
And we're basically talking
about the same problems

73
00:03:11,010 --> 00:03:15,390
again, except maybe
some things work.

74
00:03:15,390 --> 00:03:17,220
Some things don't work.

75
00:03:17,220 --> 00:03:20,550
And this talk is
about some of the--

76
00:03:20,550 --> 00:03:23,760
one of the pieces that has made
this larger enterprise work.

77
00:03:23,760 --> 00:03:28,120
And we're getting closer to the
original goals of the Dartmouth

78
00:03:28,120 --> 00:03:28,620
Conference.

79
00:03:28,620 --> 00:03:29,610
Yeah.

80
00:03:29,610 --> 00:03:32,910
Again, so this is the big gaps.

81
00:03:32,910 --> 00:03:35,400
So what eventually happened
in the field was that

82
00:03:35,400 --> 00:03:41,250
their goal of having a single
system that explained most of--

83
00:03:41,250 --> 00:03:43,650
that was able to mimic our
cognitive abilities, which

84
00:03:43,650 --> 00:03:45,933
would definitely mean
image processing,

85
00:03:45,933 --> 00:03:47,850
or image understanding
and language processing

86
00:03:47,850 --> 00:03:49,830
as well, that--

87
00:03:49,830 --> 00:03:54,240
the field got-- a single
model or a single approach

88
00:03:54,240 --> 00:03:57,120
to do all these things
was shattered by thousands

89
00:03:57,120 --> 00:03:59,040
of different research projects.

90
00:03:59,040 --> 00:04:01,440
So there was no consolidation.

91
00:04:01,440 --> 00:04:04,440
But here's another.

92
00:04:04,440 --> 00:04:06,210
This is going to
be a harder one.

93
00:04:06,210 --> 00:04:09,000
Can you tell what
is a-- this is 2009.

94
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:11,460
And this a-- this is
not a single system.

95
00:04:11,460 --> 00:04:13,620
This is a complicated
machine translation system.

96
00:04:13,620 --> 00:04:16,320
So when I started my PhD,
machine translation systems

97
00:04:16,320 --> 00:04:19,680
used to be a bit more
complicated than this, actually.

98
00:04:19,680 --> 00:04:21,690
Thousands of pipeline systems.

99
00:04:21,690 --> 00:04:23,190
You had to first
extract-- you had

100
00:04:23,190 --> 00:04:26,220
to first do word alignments that
actually looked like attention.

101
00:04:26,220 --> 00:04:28,530
It's like [INAUDIBLE] attention.

102
00:04:28,530 --> 00:04:30,900
Then, based on that, you
extracted how larger phrases

103
00:04:30,900 --> 00:04:32,088
aligned with other phrases.

104
00:04:32,088 --> 00:04:34,380
Then you had to figure out
how to-- how they-- then you

105
00:04:34,380 --> 00:04:35,467
had to teach.

106
00:04:35,467 --> 00:04:37,050
There was some machine
learning there.

107
00:04:37,050 --> 00:04:38,880
You had to teach your
model how to score them

108
00:04:38,880 --> 00:04:40,005
connecting with each other.

109
00:04:40,005 --> 00:04:42,150
So can you-- does
anybody know where

110
00:04:42,150 --> 00:04:43,880
a neural network is in this?

111
00:04:43,880 --> 00:04:51,340

112
00:04:51,340 --> 00:04:53,500
All right.

113
00:04:53,500 --> 00:04:57,160
So this is a machine
translation system from 2009.

114
00:04:57,160 --> 00:05:00,640
And CSLM is a Continuous
State Language Model

115
00:05:00,640 --> 00:05:03,280
that they would
use for re-scoring.

116
00:05:03,280 --> 00:05:05,290
So the world was
so discrete then

117
00:05:05,290 --> 00:05:07,810
that you had to call these
models Continuous State Language

118
00:05:07,810 --> 00:05:08,310
Models.

119
00:05:08,310 --> 00:05:11,800
And it was largely inspired
by the Neural Probabilistic

120
00:05:11,800 --> 00:05:13,240
Language Model by--

121
00:05:13,240 --> 00:05:14,966
oh, it doesn't appear.

122
00:05:14,966 --> 00:05:17,760
Huh.

123
00:05:17,760 --> 00:05:20,250
Sorry.

124
00:05:20,250 --> 00:05:23,190
Oh, there, the Neural
Probabilistic Language Model

125
00:05:23,190 --> 00:05:25,960
by Bengio, I think
it was, in 2003.

126
00:05:25,960 --> 00:05:28,560
And so we were--

127
00:05:28,560 --> 00:05:33,960
even in 2013, when I published a
paper on neural network language

128
00:05:33,960 --> 00:05:39,510
models, these models were still
being put into the feed forward.

129
00:05:39,510 --> 00:05:42,300
Neural network language
models were still re-scoring.

130
00:05:42,300 --> 00:05:46,230
And now, it's incredible
if you think about it, just

131
00:05:46,230 --> 00:05:49,350
in terms of consolidation, how
all of these complicated systems

132
00:05:49,350 --> 00:05:51,330
that have now been
replaced by just

133
00:05:51,330 --> 00:05:52,825
neurons that talk to each other.

134
00:05:52,825 --> 00:05:54,450
And you just learn
the rules from-- you

135
00:05:54,450 --> 00:05:58,370
just learn the rules
from data automatically.

136
00:05:58,370 --> 00:05:59,910
So it's fun to--

137
00:05:59,910 --> 00:06:02,400
it's interesting to see.

138
00:06:02,400 --> 00:06:09,730
And since then-- so this is
what the NLP 2013 conference

139
00:06:09,730 --> 00:06:10,230
was like.

140
00:06:10,230 --> 00:06:11,940
You see these different--

141
00:06:11,940 --> 00:06:14,790
these-- you can
call it verticalized

142
00:06:14,790 --> 00:06:17,850
NLPs, different areas
like morphology, dialogue,

143
00:06:17,850 --> 00:06:19,840
and discourse.

144
00:06:19,840 --> 00:06:22,520
I don't even know if people
talk about [INAUDIBLE]

145
00:06:22,520 --> 00:06:23,708
this talk tomorrow.

146
00:06:23,708 --> 00:06:25,750
I don't know if there's
a research track anymore.

147
00:06:25,750 --> 00:06:27,480
Then there is a
machine translation.

148
00:06:27,480 --> 00:06:30,190
So there's opinion mining
and sentiment analysis.

149
00:06:30,190 --> 00:06:34,540
Now models make
you angry or upset.

150
00:06:34,540 --> 00:06:37,030
And so you could see
that, just in 2013,

151
00:06:37,030 --> 00:06:40,160
the field-- even research was
divided into these smaller

152
00:06:40,160 --> 00:06:40,660
tracks.

153
00:06:40,660 --> 00:06:42,490
And everybody had
their own specific--

154
00:06:42,490 --> 00:06:47,162
they were bringing their own
specific domain information.

155
00:06:47,162 --> 00:06:49,120
And they had to specialize
in a domain in order

156
00:06:49,120 --> 00:06:50,230
to solve some task.

157
00:06:50,230 --> 00:06:51,850
And we solved tasks
to some degree.

158
00:06:51,850 --> 00:06:55,940
Machine translation-- probably
because of a lot of government

159
00:06:55,940 --> 00:06:57,940
funding as well, we have
made a lot of progress.

160
00:06:57,940 --> 00:07:00,440
And we were making practical
translation systems that were

161
00:07:00,440 --> 00:07:01,840
being deployed in the wild.

162
00:07:01,840 --> 00:07:04,360
Google Translate was a
great example of that.

163
00:07:04,360 --> 00:07:11,860
And so since then, you
have this-- we started to--

164
00:07:11,860 --> 00:07:14,920
first, we all agreed we
need distributed word

165
00:07:14,920 --> 00:07:15,640
representations.

166
00:07:15,640 --> 00:07:17,050
And you saw this.

167
00:07:17,050 --> 00:07:20,110
People probably
remember this funky

168
00:07:20,110 --> 00:07:23,920
embedding algebra, King minus
Man plus Woman equals Queen,

169
00:07:23,920 --> 00:07:24,700
from Word2Vec.

170
00:07:24,700 --> 00:07:27,760

171
00:07:27,760 --> 00:07:30,370
And we had a--

172
00:07:30,370 --> 00:07:34,630
there was a big industry
of models that actually--

173
00:07:34,630 --> 00:07:36,817
that just-- that learned
word representations.

174
00:07:36,817 --> 00:07:38,650
And these word
representations were actually

175
00:07:38,650 --> 00:07:40,810
useful in downstream tasks.

176
00:07:40,810 --> 00:07:47,620
And then came another step
in this process, where now we

177
00:07:47,620 --> 00:07:50,360
started saying, OK,
these representations--

178
00:07:50,360 --> 00:07:54,430
they're only helpful if
they're learned in context.

179
00:07:54,430 --> 00:07:59,830
So the King should change based
on context, the King of Persia,

180
00:07:59,830 --> 00:08:03,220
or the king has no clothes,
or the emperor has no clothes.

181
00:08:03,220 --> 00:08:07,690
And so we saw these--
we saw approaches

182
00:08:07,690 --> 00:08:09,700
like sequence-to-sequence
to sequence learning,

183
00:08:09,700 --> 00:08:12,460
where we started to formulate.

184
00:08:12,460 --> 00:08:15,310
We started to create these
general formulations of how

185
00:08:15,310 --> 00:08:18,220
to solve any task in NLP.

186
00:08:18,220 --> 00:08:19,810
So sequence-to-sequence
formulation--

187
00:08:19,810 --> 00:08:23,437
you can formulate
many tasks in language

188
00:08:23,437 --> 00:08:25,270
as sequence-to-sequence,
question answering,

189
00:08:25,270 --> 00:08:28,000
machine translation, dialogue.

190
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:30,310
And then, of course, we had--

191
00:08:30,310 --> 00:08:32,740
then we developed
attention, which

192
00:08:32,740 --> 00:08:35,289
was a very effective,
content-based way

193
00:08:35,289 --> 00:08:37,720
to summarize information.

194
00:08:37,720 --> 00:08:40,600
Typically, you have these
encoder-decoder architectures.

195
00:08:40,600 --> 00:08:42,105
Everybody is probably,
I'm guessing,

196
00:08:42,105 --> 00:08:44,230
familiar with encoder-decoder
architectures, right?

197
00:08:44,230 --> 00:08:48,130
So encoder-decoder architecture,
in a position on the decoder

198
00:08:48,130 --> 00:08:50,530
side, could summarize,
based on its content,

199
00:08:50,530 --> 00:08:52,748
all the information on
the source sentence.

200
00:08:52,748 --> 00:08:54,790
And this is a really
effective, content-based way

201
00:08:54,790 --> 00:08:55,990
of summarizing information.

202
00:08:55,990 --> 00:09:00,970
And what started
happening was we started--

203
00:09:00,970 --> 00:09:03,610
these general paradigms
started coming up.

204
00:09:03,610 --> 00:09:05,650
Sequence-to-sequence
learning can solve--

205
00:09:05,650 --> 00:09:07,822
it can install most
language problems

206
00:09:07,822 --> 00:09:09,280
because most language
problems have

207
00:09:09,280 --> 00:09:10,822
to deal with learning
representations

208
00:09:10,822 --> 00:09:11,980
of variables length.

209
00:09:11,980 --> 00:09:13,480
The goal is to learn
representations

210
00:09:13,480 --> 00:09:14,688
of variable-length sequences.

211
00:09:14,688 --> 00:09:17,320
And if you do that successfully,
you can then potentially

212
00:09:17,320 --> 00:09:18,130
solve that problem.

213
00:09:18,130 --> 00:09:19,930
And then attention
was an excellent way--

214
00:09:19,930 --> 00:09:23,170
a content-based way to
actually summarize information

215
00:09:23,170 --> 00:09:24,670
from some neighborhood.

216
00:09:24,670 --> 00:09:28,270

217
00:09:28,270 --> 00:09:30,340
And the major
workhorse until then

218
00:09:30,340 --> 00:09:33,520
were these recurrent
models, or LSTMs,

219
00:09:33,520 --> 00:09:38,200
where, basically, the method
was typically the same.

220
00:09:38,200 --> 00:09:40,960
You had a sentence, and
you crushed the sentence

221
00:09:40,960 --> 00:09:45,610
into a set of vectors that are
representations, one typically--

222
00:09:45,610 --> 00:09:47,230
typically, one
for each position.

223
00:09:47,230 --> 00:09:49,270
And the way LSTMs
did it was where

224
00:09:49,270 --> 00:09:51,040
they walked along the sentence.

225
00:09:51,040 --> 00:09:53,650
They ate up a
word, and then they

226
00:09:53,650 --> 00:09:57,280
summarized the entire history
into one fixed bottleneck.

227
00:09:57,280 --> 00:09:59,110
And that bottleneck
was then transmitted--

228
00:09:59,110 --> 00:10:02,230
was updated based
on the next word.

229
00:10:02,230 --> 00:10:06,757
And if you were successfully
able to learn representations,

230
00:10:06,757 --> 00:10:09,340
then we could solve these tasks
like translation summarization

231
00:10:09,340 --> 00:10:09,970
dialogue.

232
00:10:09,970 --> 00:10:11,012
So an important movement.

233
00:10:11,012 --> 00:10:13,815
And in 2020, I--

234
00:10:13,815 --> 00:10:15,940
I guess, when was the
sequence-to-sequence learning

235
00:10:15,940 --> 00:10:16,440
papers?

236
00:10:16,440 --> 00:10:18,640
2015 NeurIPS.

237
00:10:18,640 --> 00:10:21,658
Then we saw the attention
paper in-- around 2015, 2016.

238
00:10:21,658 --> 00:10:23,200
And the machine
translation community

239
00:10:23,200 --> 00:10:25,100
was the first to
respond and say,

240
00:10:25,100 --> 00:10:28,390
hey, machine translation is a
classic sequence-to-sequence

241
00:10:28,390 --> 00:10:29,590
learning problem.

242
00:10:29,590 --> 00:10:31,690
Why don't we now first
start re-scoring?

243
00:10:31,690 --> 00:10:33,405
And then can we
still build native

244
00:10:33,405 --> 00:10:35,320
to really rethink
machine translation

245
00:10:35,320 --> 00:10:37,870
with these
sequence-to-sequence models?

246
00:10:37,870 --> 00:10:39,830
And these are fantastic models.

247
00:10:39,830 --> 00:10:42,700
I don't know if you guys have
ever done these exercises on--

248
00:10:42,700 --> 00:10:45,850
LSTMs can count.

249
00:10:45,850 --> 00:10:50,830
For example, if you train
an encoder-decoder on--

250
00:10:50,830 --> 00:10:53,650
to model a to the n, b to
the n-- so you feed in n a's.

251
00:10:53,650 --> 00:10:57,340
And you ask the decoder
to predict n b's.

252
00:10:57,340 --> 00:10:59,230
And you-- actually just
a single-cell LSTM--

253
00:10:59,230 --> 00:11:01,090
if you know the
structure of an LSTM,

254
00:11:01,090 --> 00:11:03,040
there's a cell that
basically keeps--

255
00:11:03,040 --> 00:11:04,780
so it's a notion of state.

256
00:11:04,780 --> 00:11:07,150
And just a single cell
is able to actually

257
00:11:07,150 --> 00:11:08,440
just do trivial counting.

258
00:11:08,440 --> 00:11:11,680
It counts how many
a's you consumed,

259
00:11:11,680 --> 00:11:12,888
and then it decrements it.

260
00:11:12,888 --> 00:11:14,680
And then when you
consume all the-- exactly

261
00:11:14,680 --> 00:11:17,132
the same number of b's as
the number of a's, something

262
00:11:17,132 --> 00:11:18,340
lights up and says, I'm done.

263
00:11:18,340 --> 00:11:19,520
I've recognized this language.

264
00:11:19,520 --> 00:11:21,728
So you can train trivial
like a to the n, b to the n.

265
00:11:21,728 --> 00:11:23,210
And here, you have a--

266
00:11:23,210 --> 00:11:24,440
I'm sorry this is not clear.

267
00:11:24,440 --> 00:11:25,850
But you have somewhat of a--

268
00:11:25,850 --> 00:11:26,850
you have a grammar here.

269
00:11:26,850 --> 00:11:28,808
And you can see that
these are different cells.

270
00:11:28,808 --> 00:11:30,140
This is about eight cells here.

271
00:11:30,140 --> 00:11:31,730
And each one of
these cells actually

272
00:11:31,730 --> 00:11:34,490
increments its counter once
it reads a particular symbol.

273
00:11:34,490 --> 00:11:36,650
And it's able to actually
track how deep you

274
00:11:36,650 --> 00:11:40,370
are in this hierarchy
and this grammar.

275
00:11:40,370 --> 00:11:43,140
And Google, of course--

276
00:11:43,140 --> 00:11:45,320
the crowning
achievement, perhaps,

277
00:11:45,320 --> 00:11:49,980
of sequence-to-sequence models,
which I was actually right--

278
00:11:49,980 --> 00:11:53,930
I was fortunate to be
in the same cubicle

279
00:11:53,930 --> 00:11:55,940
as it was being-- as
this work was being done,

280
00:11:55,940 --> 00:11:58,040
was the Google Neural
Machine Translation System,

281
00:11:58,040 --> 00:12:00,080
where they took LSTMs.

282
00:12:00,080 --> 00:12:02,720
They added many
advancements towards a lot

283
00:12:02,720 --> 00:12:05,360
of systems, improvements to a
lot of data that Google had.

284
00:12:05,360 --> 00:12:07,040
And they produced
what you might--

285
00:12:07,040 --> 00:12:09,285
at that time, the
state of the art

286
00:12:09,285 --> 00:12:10,660
neural machine
translation system

287
00:12:10,660 --> 00:12:11,993
and sequence-to-sequence models.

288
00:12:11,993 --> 00:12:16,820
So now this big, consolidated--
this big, complicated system,

289
00:12:16,820 --> 00:12:18,800
which looked much
more complicated,

290
00:12:18,800 --> 00:12:22,160
had now become a homogeneous--
just a single homogeneous neural

291
00:12:22,160 --> 00:12:24,020
network.

292
00:12:24,020 --> 00:12:29,000
So at that time, the biggest
frustration we had was--

293
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:31,040
the LSTMs were the
primary workhorse.

294
00:12:31,040 --> 00:12:35,990
And the biggest frustration
we had was, they--

295
00:12:35,990 --> 00:12:38,380
Not only were we producing.

296
00:12:38,380 --> 00:12:40,400
Not only were we--
did we produce

297
00:12:40,400 --> 00:12:41,708
the output autoregressively.

298
00:12:41,708 --> 00:12:44,000
We were sequentially decoding
the output left-to-right.

299
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:46,490
But also, we were reading
the input sequentially.

300
00:12:46,490 --> 00:12:48,470
So you had to--

301
00:12:48,470 --> 00:12:50,780
In order to produce the
representation for the 10th

302
00:12:50,780 --> 00:12:53,840
word, you had to eat up the
first word, the second word,

303
00:12:53,840 --> 00:12:54,470
the third word.

304
00:12:54,470 --> 00:12:59,540
So that was really slow,
and not the whole--

305
00:12:59,540 --> 00:13:02,090
and another big
problem with LSTMs

306
00:13:02,090 --> 00:13:04,280
were that you have
this bottleneck

307
00:13:04,280 --> 00:13:07,160
that basically-- that
contains all the information

308
00:13:07,160 --> 00:13:08,090
about your past.

309
00:13:08,090 --> 00:13:10,550
So you have to now crush--

310
00:13:10,550 --> 00:13:13,550
you have to pack both
long-distance interactions

311
00:13:13,550 --> 00:13:15,590
that you might have
and local interactions

312
00:13:15,590 --> 00:13:19,160
through the single fixed vector
that you need to transmit.

313
00:13:19,160 --> 00:13:22,990
And sequentiality doesn't--
inhibits parallelism,

314
00:13:22,990 --> 00:13:24,947
which means that you
couldn't even read.

315
00:13:24,947 --> 00:13:27,280
The encoder couldn't even
read the sentence in parallel.

316
00:13:27,280 --> 00:13:29,560
And of course, decoding
was auto-regressive.

317
00:13:29,560 --> 00:13:32,830
So you couldn't even
write in parallel.

318
00:13:32,830 --> 00:13:36,610
And convolutions
were-- they were

319
00:13:36,610 --> 00:13:40,448
starting to emerge as
a solution, largely.

320
00:13:40,448 --> 00:13:42,490
They had been very successful
in computer vision.

321
00:13:42,490 --> 00:13:44,740
They had also figured out
how to optimize them while--

322
00:13:44,740 --> 00:13:46,120
how to make them--
how to train--

323
00:13:46,120 --> 00:13:49,330
how to make them really
fast on GPUs, where--

324
00:13:49,330 --> 00:13:52,240
because they're just basically
matrix multiplications.

325
00:13:52,240 --> 00:13:54,100
And matrix multiplication
is largely--

326
00:13:54,100 --> 00:13:55,720
it's parallelizable.

327
00:13:55,720 --> 00:13:59,890
So convolutions were a
solution to this problem

328
00:13:59,890 --> 00:14:04,120
of not being able to read in
parallel because you could-- in

329
00:14:04,120 --> 00:14:06,610
parallel, every word,
basically, produced

330
00:14:06,610 --> 00:14:10,900
its representations by
looking at its neighbors,

331
00:14:10,900 --> 00:14:12,010
its local neighbors.

332
00:14:12,010 --> 00:14:15,160
And there were some very--

333
00:14:15,160 --> 00:14:17,650
there were some breakthrough
papers, such as ByteNet,

334
00:14:17,650 --> 00:14:20,465
for machine translation,
the Convolutional Sequence 2

335
00:14:20,465 --> 00:14:22,090
Sequence model that
was contemporaneous

336
00:14:22,090 --> 00:14:24,132
to the transformer, actually
probably predated it

337
00:14:24,132 --> 00:14:26,350
by a few months, where
they used convolutions

338
00:14:26,350 --> 00:14:29,770
both in the encoder and decoder
to get good scores on machine

339
00:14:29,770 --> 00:14:32,260
translation that were better
than the Google Neural Machine

340
00:14:32,260 --> 00:14:33,250
Translation System.

341
00:14:33,250 --> 00:14:39,718
And of course, probably
the most successful

342
00:14:39,718 --> 00:14:42,010
was WaveNet, which was a
text-to-speech system that was

343
00:14:42,010 --> 00:14:43,690
state of the art at the time.

344
00:14:43,690 --> 00:14:47,710

345
00:14:47,710 --> 00:14:51,640
So convolution still had
this problem that-- one,

346
00:14:51,640 --> 00:14:53,590
I guess-- they were
parallelizable.

347
00:14:53,590 --> 00:14:57,490
But the issue was that you
still-- you couldn't directly

348
00:14:57,490 --> 00:15:01,030
capture long-distance
interactions between-- you

349
00:15:01,030 --> 00:15:03,340
couldn't directly capture
long-distance interactions

350
00:15:03,340 --> 00:15:04,090
between words.

351
00:15:04,090 --> 00:15:06,160
So if you're basically
a receptive field--

352
00:15:06,160 --> 00:15:08,530
if it's a 3 by 3--

353
00:15:08,530 --> 00:15:12,130
If it's a 1 by 3, then it
basically grows linearly

354
00:15:12,130 --> 00:15:13,780
with the factor of--
it grows linearly

355
00:15:13,780 --> 00:15:19,140
with the number of layers
each time it expands by 3.

356
00:15:19,140 --> 00:15:21,570
So you still needed a
linear number of layers

357
00:15:21,570 --> 00:15:24,005
to capture these
long-distance relationships.

358
00:15:24,005 --> 00:15:25,380
But attention, on
the other, hand

359
00:15:25,380 --> 00:15:28,440
was this really effective
mechanism that we knew was--

360
00:15:28,440 --> 00:15:31,770
that could actually get us--

361
00:15:31,770 --> 00:15:35,400
In one, it could actually
capture all the interactions

362
00:15:35,400 --> 00:15:39,060
between one word
and every other word

363
00:15:39,060 --> 00:15:40,560
using content-based addressing.

364
00:15:40,560 --> 00:15:42,720
Because convolutions
basically match--

365
00:15:42,720 --> 00:15:44,827
convolutions match
weights with parameters.

366
00:15:44,827 --> 00:15:47,160
Attention was actually able
to use content with content.

367
00:15:47,160 --> 00:15:50,280
So based on how similar
I am to my neighborhood--

368
00:15:50,280 --> 00:15:52,110
based on how similar
I am to my neighbors,

369
00:15:52,110 --> 00:15:53,652
I'm going to absorb
that information.

370
00:15:53,652 --> 00:15:56,790
And this actually-- this motif
actually appears everywhere,

371
00:15:56,790 --> 00:15:58,020
even in computer vision.

372
00:15:58,020 --> 00:16:00,450
So maybe, actually,
I can go there.

373
00:16:00,450 --> 00:16:05,100
So here's a-- in vision,
there's this approach.

374
00:16:05,100 --> 00:16:08,990
Do people here know
of non-local means?

375
00:16:08,990 --> 00:16:11,980
So in computer vision, there's
an approach called non-local

376
00:16:11,980 --> 00:16:14,770
means that's basically--

377
00:16:14,770 --> 00:16:17,950
it was originally developed
for image denoising.

378
00:16:17,950 --> 00:16:19,930
So if you want to
denoise an image patch,

379
00:16:19,930 --> 00:16:22,150
you basically-- you look
at all your neighbors,

380
00:16:22,150 --> 00:16:24,132
and you see which patch
is very similar to you.

381
00:16:24,132 --> 00:16:25,840
And based on the
similarity, you actually

382
00:16:25,840 --> 00:16:27,310
pull in that information.

383
00:16:27,310 --> 00:16:29,620
And this largely works
in images because images

384
00:16:29,620 --> 00:16:31,300
are very self-similar.

385
00:16:31,300 --> 00:16:33,460
They start sounding like,
hey, based on content,

386
00:16:33,460 --> 00:16:35,110
I want to pull in information.

387
00:16:35,110 --> 00:16:39,010
And again, there were similar--
there were approaches like

388
00:16:39,010 --> 00:16:40,930
Texture Synthesis
by Efros, where,

389
00:16:40,930 --> 00:16:44,275
if you wanted to do painting,
or if you wanted to generate

390
00:16:44,275 --> 00:16:46,900
an image, then you would look at
a patch that's similar to this

391
00:16:46,900 --> 00:16:49,610
rectangle in some other--

392
00:16:49,610 --> 00:16:51,910
in your dictionary,
or in a database that

393
00:16:51,910 --> 00:16:52,840
you have of patches.

394
00:16:52,840 --> 00:16:56,050
And then, based on what's
closest, you actually bring it.

395
00:16:56,050 --> 00:16:58,330
So you bring that patch,
and then you Paste it there.

396
00:16:58,330 --> 00:17:00,582
So these approaches
that look like attention

397
00:17:00,582 --> 00:17:01,540
were already prevalent.

398
00:17:01,540 --> 00:17:03,040
It's a very natural formulation.

399
00:17:03,040 --> 00:17:05,630

400
00:17:05,630 --> 00:17:10,520
And the ByteNow paper had shown
that this actually works really

401
00:17:10,520 --> 00:17:12,140
well for language as well.

402
00:17:12,140 --> 00:17:14,869
So the question then
was, why can't we

403
00:17:14,869 --> 00:17:16,730
then learn representations?

404
00:17:16,730 --> 00:17:19,160
Instead of doing this
source target, why can't we

405
00:17:19,160 --> 00:17:21,890
actually learn representations
by the sentence attending

406
00:17:21,890 --> 00:17:22,500
onto itself?

407
00:17:22,500 --> 00:17:24,967
So now you basically use--

408
00:17:24,967 --> 00:17:27,050
instead of attending-- a
source sentence attending

409
00:17:27,050 --> 00:17:29,175
to a target sentence, can
it just attend to itself?

410
00:17:29,175 --> 00:17:32,150
And the original goal of
actually when we wanted--

411
00:17:32,150 --> 00:17:37,340
when we were-- when we wanted to
actually do parallel decoding--

412
00:17:37,340 --> 00:17:39,680
So attention by
construction is paralyzable

413
00:17:39,680 --> 00:17:45,410
because each token can basically
construct its representations

414
00:17:45,410 --> 00:17:47,960
from its neighbors in parallel.

415
00:17:47,960 --> 00:17:51,170
And it directly captures
token-to-token interactions

416
00:17:51,170 --> 00:17:55,850
because now, of course, we'll
run into complexities of length.

417
00:17:55,850 --> 00:17:58,040
But we can-- and
we'll discuss how

418
00:17:58,040 --> 00:18:00,290
to solve some of these things
later, or overcome them.

419
00:18:00,290 --> 00:18:02,840
But you can direct-- instead
of having this linear growth

420
00:18:02,840 --> 00:18:04,160
and receptive field,
you can directly

421
00:18:04,160 --> 00:18:06,530
capture these interactions
because convolutions-- if you

422
00:18:06,530 --> 00:18:07,910
have a very, very
large receptive field,

423
00:18:07,910 --> 00:18:09,590
it gets computationally
very expensive.

424
00:18:09,590 --> 00:18:11,217
And it also had
these explicit gating

425
00:18:11,217 --> 00:18:12,800
and multiplicative
interactions, which

426
00:18:12,800 --> 00:18:16,850
we've often seen in Gated
PixelCNN, or [INAUDIBLE]..

427
00:18:16,850 --> 00:18:20,480
These explicit gated
multiplicative interactions

428
00:18:20,480 --> 00:18:23,210
have typically helped
training and have

429
00:18:23,210 --> 00:18:26,150
led to better accuracies.

430
00:18:26,150 --> 00:18:29,990
And as I mentioned, the original
motivation of why we actually

431
00:18:29,990 --> 00:18:35,170
wanted to do this was-- we
said, hey, so the LSTMs are--

432
00:18:35,170 --> 00:18:36,560
we have good
translation systems.

433
00:18:36,560 --> 00:18:39,140
But the problem is that,
actually, both reading

434
00:18:39,140 --> 00:18:42,710
and writing, sequentially-- can
we actually do both in parallel?

435
00:18:42,710 --> 00:18:46,670
So we wanted to read the
German sentence in parallel

436
00:18:46,670 --> 00:18:50,600
and then translate it, and then
also write in parallel by that.

437
00:18:50,600 --> 00:18:53,788
Instead of actually decoding
it autoregressively, can you

438
00:18:53,788 --> 00:18:55,580
decode it-- instead of
decoding it in time,

439
00:18:55,580 --> 00:18:57,320
can you decode it in height?

440
00:18:57,320 --> 00:18:59,420
So you first spit out one word.

441
00:18:59,420 --> 00:19:02,360
Or you spit out all the words,
and you iteratively refine them.

442
00:19:02,360 --> 00:19:06,530
And this was-- this turned out
to be very, very challenging

443
00:19:06,530 --> 00:19:09,453
and hasn't been solved
successfully until today.

444
00:19:09,453 --> 00:19:11,870
Because the biggest challenge,
essentially, is, when you--

445
00:19:11,870 --> 00:19:13,640
whenever you're
decoding, essentially,

446
00:19:13,640 --> 00:19:16,400
as you predict a word, you bend
the probability distribution

447
00:19:16,400 --> 00:19:18,110
that then nails down--

448
00:19:18,110 --> 00:19:20,630
narrows down what you're
going to predict later on.

449
00:19:20,630 --> 00:19:24,170
And the ordering that
allows you to basically--

450
00:19:24,170 --> 00:19:26,450
the ordering that allows
you to nail these modes

451
00:19:26,450 --> 00:19:28,340
was very hard to learn.

452
00:19:28,340 --> 00:19:31,190
So imposing left-right
ordering is much easier

453
00:19:31,190 --> 00:19:33,020
than actually not
having one and having

454
00:19:33,020 --> 00:19:34,730
to learn it as you're decoding.

455
00:19:34,730 --> 00:19:37,040
So the original approach
just didn't work.

456
00:19:37,040 --> 00:19:41,330
But then we still
had our salvation

457
00:19:41,330 --> 00:19:42,890
in being able to
read parallelly.

458
00:19:42,890 --> 00:19:44,180
So we said, all right,
let's take this back

459
00:19:44,180 --> 00:19:45,430
to the encoder-decoder models.

460
00:19:45,430 --> 00:19:47,840

461
00:19:47,840 --> 00:19:51,260
And unlike-- at that time,
there were a few formulations.

462
00:19:51,260 --> 00:19:52,820
So we had this--

463
00:19:52,820 --> 00:19:55,700
the original formulation
of attention from Graves.

464
00:19:55,700 --> 00:19:58,850
Then we had the Additive
attention formulation.

465
00:19:58,850 --> 00:20:01,880
And we took the Dot-Product
attention formulation,

466
00:20:01,880 --> 00:20:05,183
largely because it
allowed us to do--

467
00:20:05,183 --> 00:20:07,100
because it allowed us
to actually do attention

468
00:20:07,100 --> 00:20:09,080
as a matrix multiplication.

469
00:20:09,080 --> 00:20:11,450
And oftentimes, some of
the biggest constraints

470
00:20:11,450 --> 00:20:14,780
that actually-- physics
is such a big constraint

471
00:20:14,780 --> 00:20:18,860
in neural networks
that, if you can

472
00:20:18,860 --> 00:20:24,017
make your architecture amenable
to modern accelerators,

473
00:20:24,017 --> 00:20:25,850
you have a much better
chance of succeeding.

474
00:20:25,850 --> 00:20:27,980
And Dot-Product attention
could be expressed

475
00:20:27,980 --> 00:20:29,690
as a matrix multiplication.

476
00:20:29,690 --> 00:20:33,530
And there are already
kernels for being

477
00:20:33,530 --> 00:20:36,980
able to do matrix multiplication
very effectively on the GPU.

478
00:20:36,980 --> 00:20:39,398
So the formulation was--
all right, so now we have--

479
00:20:39,398 --> 00:20:40,940
similar to the
Dot-Product attention,

480
00:20:40,940 --> 00:20:43,520
we had a scaling
factor, simply because--

481
00:20:43,520 --> 00:20:45,650
if the Dot-Product
actually becomes too big,

482
00:20:45,650 --> 00:20:48,140
and you can solve it under
certain assumptions of mean

483
00:20:48,140 --> 00:20:50,647
and variance in the
representations,

484
00:20:50,647 --> 00:20:51,480
you can [INAUDIBLE].

485
00:20:51,480 --> 00:20:54,830
Oh, it hasn't updated, actually.

486
00:20:54,830 --> 00:20:56,210
Yeah.

487
00:20:56,210 --> 00:20:57,800
So our formulation
is-- basically,

488
00:20:57,800 --> 00:21:03,650
you have your queries,
which, what you end up

489
00:21:03,650 --> 00:21:05,450
doing is-- if you
have a position,

490
00:21:05,450 --> 00:21:08,270
you first project
it into queries.

491
00:21:08,270 --> 00:21:10,280
And then the same token--

492
00:21:10,280 --> 00:21:11,780
the representation
of the same token

493
00:21:11,780 --> 00:21:14,450
gets projected into
also keys and values.

494
00:21:14,450 --> 00:21:17,330
And the first-- the
query determines

495
00:21:17,330 --> 00:21:21,890
how much you're actually going
to pull from all these keys.

496
00:21:21,890 --> 00:21:24,740
So you first do a Dot-Product
of the query with every key.

497
00:21:24,740 --> 00:21:26,780
And then, based on
that, you combine,

498
00:21:26,780 --> 00:21:30,380
or you pool the content
of all these positions

499
00:21:30,380 --> 00:21:33,980
based on what the score
was after normalizing

500
00:21:33,980 --> 00:21:35,180
it using a softmax.

501
00:21:35,180 --> 00:21:39,830
So in some sense, you can
think of self-attention also as

502
00:21:39,830 --> 00:21:42,200
a content-based
pooling mechanism.

503
00:21:42,200 --> 00:21:45,980
And the scaling factor
basically avoids you--

504
00:21:45,980 --> 00:21:49,280
it saved us from these logits
actually blowing up in training,

505
00:21:49,280 --> 00:21:50,360
becoming unstable.

506
00:21:50,360 --> 00:21:53,780
And on the decoder side,
you could trivially--

507
00:21:53,780 --> 00:21:56,940
you can trivially
implement causality by just

508
00:21:56,940 --> 00:22:01,020
adding an attention mask.

509
00:22:01,020 --> 00:22:04,440
And what this-- where this
brings us is that-- all right.

510
00:22:04,440 --> 00:22:07,620
So now we've solved.

511
00:22:07,620 --> 00:22:09,390
So a caveat on the FLOPs.

512
00:22:09,390 --> 00:22:10,900
We'll actually cover this later.

513
00:22:10,900 --> 00:22:16,410
But now what we have is a
mechanism that's parallelizable.

514
00:22:16,410 --> 00:22:17,740
It gives you direct content.

515
00:22:17,740 --> 00:22:19,365
It gives you direct
token interactions,

516
00:22:19,365 --> 00:22:22,227
which we'll-- and that we
believe is going to help you

517
00:22:22,227 --> 00:22:24,810
actually learn and model these
relationships between the words

518
00:22:24,810 --> 00:22:26,250
better.

519
00:22:26,250 --> 00:22:27,750
And the complexity
of self-attention

520
00:22:27,750 --> 00:22:30,780
is faster than convolutions
because convolutions

521
00:22:30,780 --> 00:22:32,460
are quadratic in the number.

522
00:22:32,460 --> 00:22:34,677
They're quadratic in
the number of channels

523
00:22:34,677 --> 00:22:36,510
and the number of-- in
the hidden dimension.

524
00:22:36,510 --> 00:22:38,160
But self-attention is
quadratic in the length.

525
00:22:38,160 --> 00:22:40,140
So if your length is not much
more than your hidden dimension,

526
00:22:40,140 --> 00:22:41,610
you've actually saved on FLOPs.

527
00:22:41,610 --> 00:22:44,370
Now, this is not quite
a complete picture

528
00:22:44,370 --> 00:22:46,680
because not all FLOPs are equal.

529
00:22:46,680 --> 00:22:48,300
And we'll talk
about this later on.

530
00:22:48,300 --> 00:22:50,830

531
00:22:50,830 --> 00:22:54,120
And now, when you put
everything together, what we--

532
00:22:54,120 --> 00:22:58,290
basically, we took the basis.

533
00:22:58,290 --> 00:23:00,450
This has a very
strong similarity

534
00:23:00,450 --> 00:23:02,550
to the ResNet
architecture, actually.

535
00:23:02,550 --> 00:23:04,110
So if you look at ResNets--

536
00:23:04,110 --> 00:23:06,240
so in ResNets, you
have contraction.

537
00:23:06,240 --> 00:23:08,580
You have spatial mixing
with convolutions.

538
00:23:08,580 --> 00:23:11,100
And then you have
the expansion again.

539
00:23:11,100 --> 00:23:13,200
The transformer--
if you just adjust--

540
00:23:13,200 --> 00:23:14,970
if you just move
it one step down,

541
00:23:14,970 --> 00:23:16,488
it's very-- it's analogous.

542
00:23:16,488 --> 00:23:17,280
You have attention.

543
00:23:17,280 --> 00:23:18,780
Then you have
expansion-contraction.

544
00:23:18,780 --> 00:23:21,112
But there is a
difference in where

545
00:23:21,112 --> 00:23:22,320
the residual connections are.

546
00:23:22,320 --> 00:23:26,940
But it's a very similar
basic building block

547
00:23:26,940 --> 00:23:28,230
with the residual connections.

548
00:23:28,230 --> 00:23:30,390
And you have these
contractions and expansions.

549
00:23:30,390 --> 00:23:33,150
And then the
transformer-- those were--

550
00:23:33,150 --> 00:23:35,760
that was multi-head attention
with expansion-contraction,

551
00:23:35,760 --> 00:23:38,670
which was in the
feed forward layers.

552
00:23:38,670 --> 00:23:42,570
And then one challenge
with the attention.

553
00:23:42,570 --> 00:23:43,950
LSTMs can count.

554
00:23:43,950 --> 00:23:46,800
In fact, they can count.

555
00:23:46,800 --> 00:23:49,890
They can learn interesting
temporal patterns.

556
00:23:49,890 --> 00:23:53,760
But attention is
permutation invariant.

557
00:23:53,760 --> 00:23:55,958
So we had to actually
add position.

558
00:23:55,958 --> 00:23:57,500
We had to add position
information so

559
00:23:57,500 --> 00:23:59,000
that we could learn ordering.

560
00:23:59,000 --> 00:24:01,370
So we add position
information at the input,

561
00:24:01,370 --> 00:24:04,280
which gets transmitted
to the other layers

562
00:24:04,280 --> 00:24:06,950
through the residual
connections.

563
00:24:06,950 --> 00:24:09,470
And the original paper--

564
00:24:09,470 --> 00:24:10,550
we had post-LayerNorm.

565
00:24:10,550 --> 00:24:13,280
But later on, we realized
that, as we actually

566
00:24:13,280 --> 00:24:16,820
make the model deeper,
post-LayerNorm doesn't

567
00:24:16,820 --> 00:24:18,140
allow you to train effectively.

568
00:24:18,140 --> 00:24:19,130
So we had to--

569
00:24:19,130 --> 00:24:21,290
then we used a
pre-LayerNorm formulation,

570
00:24:21,290 --> 00:24:24,260
which was also observed in
the original ResNet papers.

571
00:24:24,260 --> 00:24:27,770
And so the model is
basically-- all right.

572
00:24:27,770 --> 00:24:30,680
You've got your input and
you have spatial mixing.

573
00:24:30,680 --> 00:24:33,110
Spatial mixing to attention,
feed forward layers.

574
00:24:33,110 --> 00:24:34,370
And this repeats.

575
00:24:34,370 --> 00:24:37,760
And the difference
on the decoder side

576
00:24:37,760 --> 00:24:43,610
is that you also now have
encoder-decoder attention

577
00:24:43,610 --> 00:24:46,010
at every layer.

578
00:24:46,010 --> 00:24:47,420
If there's any questions-- yeah?

579
00:24:47,420 --> 00:24:48,200
Yes.

580
00:24:48,200 --> 00:24:50,210
What was your intuition
behind the [INAUDIBLE]??

581
00:24:50,210 --> 00:24:51,480
Post-LayerNorm?

582
00:24:51,480 --> 00:24:52,190
Oh.

583
00:24:52,190 --> 00:24:57,800
So it ended up-- so if you
do post-LayerNorm, then--

584
00:24:57,800 --> 00:25:00,500
actually, let's-- do
I have that slide?

585
00:25:00,500 --> 00:25:01,138
Let me check.

586
00:25:01,138 --> 00:25:02,180
Probably I've deleted it.

587
00:25:02,180 --> 00:25:05,690
But if you do
post-LayerNorm, then you

588
00:25:05,690 --> 00:25:09,800
are basically squashing both the
residual and the additive parts.

589
00:25:09,800 --> 00:25:11,937
So when you-- so your
activations from the lower

590
00:25:11,937 --> 00:25:14,270
layers keep getting-- keep
going through the LayerNorms.

591
00:25:14,270 --> 00:25:16,430
But in pre-LayerNorm,
you're only--

592
00:25:16,430 --> 00:25:18,590
only your residual path
has a LayerNorm, which

593
00:25:18,590 --> 00:25:21,770
means your activations all the
way from the bottom of the model

594
00:25:21,770 --> 00:25:22,340
are free.

595
00:25:22,340 --> 00:25:24,420
They're untouched, and
they can pass through.

596
00:25:24,420 --> 00:25:24,920
Yeah.

597
00:25:24,920 --> 00:25:30,990

598
00:25:30,990 --> 00:25:34,050
So until this point,
we haven't discussed

599
00:25:34,050 --> 00:25:36,480
why did we-- we
haven't discussed

600
00:25:36,480 --> 00:25:39,900
multi-head attention, which
ended up being very important.

601
00:25:39,900 --> 00:25:43,320
So one of the problems
with attention is that--

602
00:25:43,320 --> 00:25:47,670
imagine if you wanted to--

603
00:25:47,670 --> 00:25:50,010
so oftentimes, language
is about understanding

604
00:25:50,010 --> 00:25:51,060
who did what to whom.

605
00:25:51,060 --> 00:25:55,020
So in this case, the cat
licked the owner's hands.

606
00:25:55,020 --> 00:25:57,090
So who licked what?

607
00:25:57,090 --> 00:25:58,320
The cat licked the owner.

608
00:25:58,320 --> 00:26:01,170
So now, if you actually
want to combine information

609
00:26:01,170 --> 00:26:04,440
from these two slots, these
positions, these vectors,

610
00:26:04,440 --> 00:26:06,360
then the best you
could do with attention

611
00:26:06,360 --> 00:26:08,640
is 0.5, 0.5 with the single
layer at half probability,

612
00:26:08,640 --> 00:26:09,420
half probability.

613
00:26:09,420 --> 00:26:11,430
But then they get
mushed together.

614
00:26:11,430 --> 00:26:14,280
But now imagine the strength
that a convolution has.

615
00:26:14,280 --> 00:26:18,400
It can actually have a--

616
00:26:18,400 --> 00:26:20,770
That actually should have--

617
00:26:20,770 --> 00:26:21,400
oh, OK.

618
00:26:21,400 --> 00:26:24,640
Well, I think the point
will still come across.

619
00:26:24,640 --> 00:26:27,610
So now what a convolution
can do is-- because it has--

620
00:26:27,610 --> 00:26:30,610
it basically applies--
essentially, a convolution--

621
00:26:30,610 --> 00:26:32,835
in this case, it's a 5 by 1.

622
00:26:32,835 --> 00:26:34,210
And all it really
does is it just

623
00:26:34,210 --> 00:26:38,470
applies a different linear
transformation at each position.

624
00:26:38,470 --> 00:26:40,480
So it can take any--

625
00:26:40,480 --> 00:26:43,690
and because these linear
transformations are different,

626
00:26:43,690 --> 00:26:45,917
it can-- the first linear
transformation can learn.

627
00:26:45,917 --> 00:26:48,250
I'm going to take a little
bit of information from here.

628
00:26:48,250 --> 00:26:49,910
I'm going to take a little
bit of information from here.

629
00:26:49,910 --> 00:26:51,940
And I'm going to
put them together.

630
00:26:51,940 --> 00:26:54,610
And the attention-- the best
way that you could actually

631
00:26:54,610 --> 00:26:56,160
just do this is best
by averaging out

632
00:26:56,160 --> 00:26:57,040
[INAUDIBLE] these things.

633
00:26:57,040 --> 00:26:58,750
But having different
linear transformations

634
00:26:58,750 --> 00:27:00,800
allows you to take a part
of the embedding here,

635
00:27:00,800 --> 00:27:03,612
a part of the embedding here,
mix it up, and then maybe put it

636
00:27:03,612 --> 00:27:06,070
together without actually then
interfering with each other.

637
00:27:06,070 --> 00:27:08,790
And multi-head attention,
which is a bit like--

638
00:27:08,790 --> 00:27:10,930
basically, a
multi-tape-- multi-head

639
00:27:10,930 --> 00:27:17,110
of a multi-head Turing machine
with different read-write heads.

640
00:27:17,110 --> 00:27:20,540
Essentially, it allows-- you
start getting you that property

641
00:27:20,540 --> 00:27:24,980
back, where now what you
do is you essentially--

642
00:27:24,980 --> 00:27:27,350
you now-- you bring
back the ability

643
00:27:27,350 --> 00:27:29,330
to select different
parts of the input.

644
00:27:29,330 --> 00:27:31,040
So you chop up the
hidden dimension

645
00:27:31,040 --> 00:27:32,570
into independent pieces.

646
00:27:32,570 --> 00:27:35,705
And then each one of them
is now able to do attention.

647
00:27:35,705 --> 00:27:38,330
So now you can have probability
1 in this place and probability

648
00:27:38,330 --> 00:27:41,390
1 in this other sub space,
instead of having 0.5, 0.5.

649
00:27:41,390 --> 00:27:44,750
So now you don't have to
get these averaging effects.

650
00:27:44,750 --> 00:27:47,630
You can actually be selective.

651
00:27:47,630 --> 00:27:51,170
And also, for
computational reasons, we--

652
00:27:51,170 --> 00:27:54,050
instead of actually having
eight attention layers of--

653
00:27:54,050 --> 00:27:57,082
or six attention heads
of d dimensions, we had--

654
00:27:57,082 --> 00:27:58,790
or eight attention
heads of d dimensions,

655
00:27:58,790 --> 00:28:01,730
we had eight attention
heads of d by 8 dimensions.

656
00:28:01,730 --> 00:28:04,550

657
00:28:04,550 --> 00:28:06,440
So we wouldn't incur any more--

658
00:28:06,440 --> 00:28:09,762
we wouldn't incur any
more any more FLOPs

659
00:28:09,762 --> 00:28:10,970
for the same amount of FLOPs.

660
00:28:10,970 --> 00:28:13,640
But that's only half the story
because the attention heads

661
00:28:13,640 --> 00:28:15,530
themselves turn out
to be quite expensive,

662
00:28:15,530 --> 00:28:17,090
which then later on
had to be-- there

663
00:28:17,090 --> 00:28:21,170
were improvements that
needed to be made.

664
00:28:21,170 --> 00:28:26,180
And the most important part--
probably the most important

665
00:28:26,180 --> 00:28:30,230
result was that,
with the transformer,

666
00:28:30,230 --> 00:28:33,920
we were able to outperform
previous ensemble

667
00:28:33,920 --> 00:28:35,128
models as well.

668
00:28:35,128 --> 00:28:36,920
And that was very, very
exciting that, hey,

669
00:28:36,920 --> 00:28:39,830
the single model actually
is able to outperform

670
00:28:39,830 --> 00:28:42,150
previous ensemble models.

671
00:28:42,150 --> 00:28:43,560
And not only that.

672
00:28:43,560 --> 00:28:48,172
And this is a machine
translation WMT 2014

673
00:28:48,172 --> 00:28:49,880
English-German and
English-French machine

674
00:28:49,880 --> 00:28:51,470
translation tasks.

675
00:28:51,470 --> 00:28:55,610
And not only were we able
to do it in less FLOPs,

676
00:28:55,610 --> 00:28:58,670
but also, these--

677
00:28:58,670 --> 00:29:03,668
it was very clear that this
was a very general model.

678
00:29:03,668 --> 00:29:05,210
We immediately
applied it to parsing,

679
00:29:05,210 --> 00:29:10,940
and we were able to get, with a
small model, excellent results.

680
00:29:10,940 --> 00:29:15,770
So in some sense, we were--

681
00:29:15,770 --> 00:29:18,635
this was very exciting because
this meant that, all right, now

682
00:29:18,635 --> 00:29:21,010
this consolidation that we're
trying to go for in machine

683
00:29:21,010 --> 00:29:22,760
learning-- we probably
have a model that's

684
00:29:22,760 --> 00:29:24,370
more general than
what we had before.

685
00:29:24,370 --> 00:29:27,070
And we can now throw
it at different--

686
00:29:27,070 --> 00:29:29,260
maybe we can now throw
it at different problems.

687
00:29:29,260 --> 00:29:30,580
And ultimately, why?

688
00:29:30,580 --> 00:29:36,220
Because it would be helpful
to have a single model

689
00:29:36,220 --> 00:29:38,740
that's able to combine
representations from speech,

690
00:29:38,740 --> 00:29:40,870
images, and language.

691
00:29:40,870 --> 00:29:43,870
And if you had a general
substrate that worked well

692
00:29:43,870 --> 00:29:45,940
in all tasks, then
potentially we

693
00:29:45,940 --> 00:29:47,770
could get to the single
multimodal model.

694
00:29:47,770 --> 00:29:50,560

695
00:29:50,560 --> 00:29:54,220
Sometimes, interpretability
is like tea leaves.

696
00:29:54,220 --> 00:29:56,900
It's like reading tea leaves.

697
00:29:56,900 --> 00:29:58,270
So one should be careful.

698
00:29:58,270 --> 00:30:01,420
But it was nice that
the attention by itself

699
00:30:01,420 --> 00:30:02,950
can give you some
interpretability.

700
00:30:02,950 --> 00:30:07,240
And we were able to see how
some of these attention heads

701
00:30:07,240 --> 00:30:10,127
or some of these
attention mechanisms

702
00:30:10,127 --> 00:30:12,460
were actually able to learn
long-distance relationships.

703
00:30:12,460 --> 00:30:15,100
Some actually learned to be--

704
00:30:15,100 --> 00:30:19,000
early on in the transformer,
we saw this generally invariant

705
00:30:19,000 --> 00:30:20,853
pattern, where some
of the attention heads

706
00:30:20,853 --> 00:30:23,020
basically turned out to
just look like convolutions.

707
00:30:23,020 --> 00:30:25,702
They were just they were just
pulling in local information.

708
00:30:25,702 --> 00:30:27,910
There's, of course, now
being much more advanced work

709
00:30:27,910 --> 00:30:30,160
with some of the
mechanistic interpretability

710
00:30:30,160 --> 00:30:33,640
stuff with grokking
and the stuff that

711
00:30:33,640 --> 00:30:37,010
is happening at Anthropic,
which is where they're learning.

712
00:30:37,010 --> 00:30:40,830
Now they're actually learning
how to interpret these induction

713
00:30:40,830 --> 00:30:41,330
heads.

714
00:30:41,330 --> 00:30:42,590
So it's interesting.

715
00:30:42,590 --> 00:30:47,090
But we were able to see
some anecdotal evidence

716
00:30:47,090 --> 00:30:49,580
of these heads actually
performing very, very distinct

717
00:30:49,580 --> 00:30:52,700
and clear actions.

718
00:30:52,700 --> 00:30:55,550
So if there's any
more questions, then--

719
00:30:55,550 --> 00:30:56,510
no more?

720
00:30:56,510 --> 00:30:57,740
I'll pause for a second.

721
00:30:57,740 --> 00:30:58,240
Yeah?

722
00:30:58,240 --> 00:30:59,510
Do you buy the research from--

723
00:30:59,510 --> 00:31:01,130
that it's the
induction heads that

724
00:31:01,130 --> 00:31:05,270
are causing the in-context
learning [INAUDIBLE]??

725
00:31:05,270 --> 00:31:05,770
Yeah.

726
00:31:05,770 --> 00:31:07,097
It's hard to tell because--

727
00:31:07,097 --> 00:31:09,430
so from what-- I haven't
looked at the most recent work.

728
00:31:09,430 --> 00:31:11,967
But they've solved this
issue of superposition.

729
00:31:11,967 --> 00:31:12,550
Is that right?

730
00:31:12,550 --> 00:31:15,190
So now, with having solved that,
they're able to roughly-- does

731
00:31:15,190 --> 00:31:16,607
that roughly mean
that now they're

732
00:31:16,607 --> 00:31:19,330
able-- they'll be able to
assign distinguishing features

733
00:31:19,330 --> 00:31:22,900
to each one of these sets
and be able to explain it?

734
00:31:22,900 --> 00:31:27,060
From what I understand-- or the
in-context learning part is--

735
00:31:27,060 --> 00:31:29,538
is that-- is it that
they have to show it?

736
00:31:29,538 --> 00:31:32,080
Or is it that they're saying
that in-context learning happens

737
00:31:32,080 --> 00:31:33,070
because of induction heads?

738
00:31:33,070 --> 00:31:33,280
Yeah.

739
00:31:33,280 --> 00:31:33,780
The latter.

740
00:31:33,780 --> 00:31:35,260
Oh, it's the latter.

741
00:31:35,260 --> 00:31:36,130
Yeah.

742
00:31:36,130 --> 00:31:39,970
It's not clear because--

743
00:31:39,970 --> 00:31:40,540
yeah.

744
00:31:40,540 --> 00:31:44,550
I think there's probably
many, many kinds of--

745
00:31:44,550 --> 00:31:47,860
in-context learning is shown to
work in so many different tasks

746
00:31:47,860 --> 00:31:48,610
that--

747
00:31:48,610 --> 00:31:50,890
and actually, I haven't
followed this quite well.

748
00:31:50,890 --> 00:31:51,700
I don't know if--

749
00:31:51,700 --> 00:31:52,930
I don't know specifically--

750
00:31:52,930 --> 00:31:55,510
What do the induction
heads typically do?

751
00:31:55,510 --> 00:31:57,192
What kinds of
properties do they have?

752
00:31:57,192 --> 00:31:59,275
Do you know what kinds of
mechanisms do they have?

753
00:31:59,275 --> 00:32:00,010
OK.

754
00:32:00,010 --> 00:32:01,210
So yeah.

755
00:32:01,210 --> 00:32:03,050
So since both of us
don't know this really,

756
00:32:03,050 --> 00:32:06,400
really well, I won't be
able to go very far here.

757
00:32:06,400 --> 00:32:09,420
But I'm not sure
if they've gotten

758
00:32:09,420 --> 00:32:11,170
to the point where
they're able to explain

759
00:32:11,170 --> 00:32:13,360
most of in-context learning
because of induction heads.

760
00:32:13,360 --> 00:32:14,740
From what I understand,
they might have.

761
00:32:14,740 --> 00:32:15,370
Yeah.

762
00:32:15,370 --> 00:32:19,650
Does anybody know about
the induction heads?

763
00:32:19,650 --> 00:32:20,150
OK.

764
00:32:20,150 --> 00:32:25,430

765
00:32:25,430 --> 00:32:28,850
Over the years-- so
there have been a few--

766
00:32:28,850 --> 00:32:31,520
there have been many papers.

767
00:32:31,520 --> 00:32:33,980
But there have
been a few changes

768
00:32:33,980 --> 00:32:35,330
that have been important.

769
00:32:35,330 --> 00:32:38,780
There have been a few
changes that have stuck.

770
00:32:38,780 --> 00:32:40,760
And the new
transformers typically

771
00:32:40,760 --> 00:32:43,370
have these improvements.

772
00:32:43,370 --> 00:32:48,110
And you'll go from bottom
to top with some of them

773
00:32:48,110 --> 00:32:50,990
and see which ones
have actually stuck.

774
00:32:50,990 --> 00:32:52,520
So we started with the first.

775
00:32:52,520 --> 00:32:56,240
One of the biggest problems with
self-attention was that it was--

776
00:32:56,240 --> 00:32:59,660
that self-attention itself
is permutation invariant.

777
00:32:59,660 --> 00:33:02,630

778
00:33:02,630 --> 00:33:05,180
You need to do position
information in order

779
00:33:05,180 --> 00:33:08,060
for it to learn some kind
of temporal structure.

780
00:33:08,060 --> 00:33:10,910
And in the original transformer,
we used these sinusoids.

781
00:33:10,910 --> 00:33:14,390
And we had hoped that it would
actually learn relative position

782
00:33:14,390 --> 00:33:17,060
encodings because you could
decompose the position

783
00:33:17,060 --> 00:33:19,250
encoding of another--

784
00:33:19,250 --> 00:33:22,590
you could decompose the position
embedding of another position

785
00:33:22,590 --> 00:33:27,870
as some linear function of
the previous one, and some--

786
00:33:27,870 --> 00:33:29,728
and another factor,
which is-- which

787
00:33:29,728 --> 00:33:31,770
depends on the relative
distance between the two.

788
00:33:31,770 --> 00:33:33,600
But that didn't happen.

789
00:33:33,600 --> 00:33:38,740
Learned position encodings in
the original paper did as well.

790
00:33:38,740 --> 00:33:42,810
And so we were not
quite able to get the--

791
00:33:42,810 --> 00:33:45,780
we were not quite able to get
these model relative distances

792
00:33:45,780 --> 00:33:46,800
using the sinusoids.

793
00:33:46,800 --> 00:33:50,010
So then a couple of
important-- and this

794
00:33:50,010 --> 00:33:51,600
is a very biased sample.

795
00:33:51,600 --> 00:33:56,520
But I think it generally covers
a large category of these--

796
00:33:56,520 --> 00:33:58,890
it covers a large set of papers.

797
00:33:58,890 --> 00:34:03,090
There's roughly
three categories.

798
00:34:03,090 --> 00:34:06,480
And all of them are now
explicitly learning--

799
00:34:06,480 --> 00:34:10,320
explicitly for learning
relative embeddings.

800
00:34:10,320 --> 00:34:13,199
So in the relative
position transformer,

801
00:34:13,199 --> 00:34:16,889
we had an embedding for every
pair of relative positions.

802
00:34:16,889 --> 00:34:18,667
And using that, we
basically then--

803
00:34:18,667 --> 00:34:20,250
we did a Dot-Product
of that embedding

804
00:34:20,250 --> 00:34:22,110
with the query that
produced a logit that

805
00:34:22,110 --> 00:34:25,260
was-- that modulated according
to the relative distance.

806
00:34:25,260 --> 00:34:27,540
And we found this
to be extremely--

807
00:34:27,540 --> 00:34:31,469
we found this to be extremely
useful for translation.

808
00:34:31,469 --> 00:34:34,440
And I'll show it also in music.

809
00:34:34,440 --> 00:34:38,100
Another-- maybe a
simplification--

810
00:34:38,100 --> 00:34:40,530
this is the Alibi paper,
where this is non-parametric.

811
00:34:40,530 --> 00:34:42,540
These are not learned,
where, instead

812
00:34:42,540 --> 00:34:44,760
of an embedding for
every pair of positions,

813
00:34:44,760 --> 00:34:47,204
you actually have a single bias.

814
00:34:47,204 --> 00:34:49,830
So you basically just add
a single bias to the logit.

815
00:34:49,830 --> 00:34:54,420
And you can either
learn it, or you can use

816
00:34:54,420 --> 00:34:56,440
a heuristic, which Alibi did.

817
00:34:56,440 --> 00:34:59,340
And one other advantage about
relative position encodings

818
00:34:59,340 --> 00:35:00,840
is that they could
potentially allow

819
00:35:00,840 --> 00:35:03,960
you to extrapolate to new-- to
longer sequence lengths, which

820
00:35:03,960 --> 00:35:08,600
you couldn't do with
absolute position encodings.

821
00:35:08,600 --> 00:35:10,140
I'm curious about the room--

822
00:35:10,140 --> 00:35:11,760
about what the room thinks here.

823
00:35:11,760 --> 00:35:16,380
But I believe that the latest
incarnation of relative position

824
00:35:16,380 --> 00:35:18,120
encodings, where this is--

825
00:35:18,120 --> 00:35:21,630
I believe it's called the row
former, where they basically

826
00:35:21,630 --> 00:35:26,640
just rotate the embedding
with every pair of dimensions

827
00:35:26,640 --> 00:35:28,200
a little bit.

828
00:35:28,200 --> 00:35:30,330
And the angle of
rotation depends

829
00:35:30,330 --> 00:35:32,430
on your actual
absolute distance.

830
00:35:32,430 --> 00:35:35,790
But what ends up happening
is, when you do the attention

831
00:35:35,790 --> 00:35:38,250
operation, you end up
getting relative-- you end up

832
00:35:38,250 --> 00:35:40,560
basically getting
an effect where

833
00:35:40,560 --> 00:35:42,990
you're modulating the logit
based on relative distance.

834
00:35:42,990 --> 00:35:46,500
So now what's remarkable
about this approach, what's--

835
00:35:46,500 --> 00:35:49,410
it combines the
best of both worlds.

836
00:35:49,410 --> 00:35:51,000
It's absolute
position encodings.

837
00:35:51,000 --> 00:35:52,860
Relative position
encodings had a couple

838
00:35:52,860 --> 00:35:57,090
of challenges in that you had
to maintain an extra logit for--

839
00:35:57,090 --> 00:35:58,485
or an embedding for every pair.

840
00:35:58,485 --> 00:35:59,610
So there was a lot of them.

841
00:35:59,610 --> 00:36:02,340
So it ended up
increasing your memory.

842
00:36:02,340 --> 00:36:04,590
Here-- these are actually
absolute position encodings.

843
00:36:04,590 --> 00:36:06,240
But they give
you-- they ended up

844
00:36:06,240 --> 00:36:09,510
giving you the relative
modulation in the attention

845
00:36:09,510 --> 00:36:10,620
operation that you needed.

846
00:36:10,620 --> 00:36:15,420
And I believe the consensus is
that this is the most successful

847
00:36:15,420 --> 00:36:16,343
position encoding.

848
00:36:16,343 --> 00:36:17,010
Is that correct?

849
00:36:17,010 --> 00:36:18,660
Or are there-- is there--

850
00:36:18,660 --> 00:36:23,535
are there others that people--
is that the consensus?

851
00:36:23,535 --> 00:36:24,920
OK.

852
00:36:24,920 --> 00:36:29,360
So it looks like-- so I
would say that these relative

853
00:36:29,360 --> 00:36:30,605
rotations are from--

854
00:36:30,605 --> 00:36:34,760
the approach that's in
the reformer is likely--

855
00:36:34,760 --> 00:36:37,902
is basically an actual
new, genuine improvement

856
00:36:37,902 --> 00:36:39,860
that is now going to stay
with the transformer.

857
00:36:39,860 --> 00:36:42,260
And it has all the
great properties

858
00:36:42,260 --> 00:36:44,323
of what you would want.

859
00:36:44,323 --> 00:36:45,740
It's an absolute
position encoding

860
00:36:45,740 --> 00:36:47,323
that gives you
relative effects, which

861
00:36:47,323 --> 00:36:49,460
is what we originally wanted.

862
00:36:49,460 --> 00:36:54,950
And one-- and to emphasize
that we needed relative--

863
00:36:54,950 --> 00:36:58,940

864
00:36:58,940 --> 00:37:03,380
emphasize two things, one, that
modeling interesting temporal

865
00:37:03,380 --> 00:37:06,560
relationships, which is-- which
are really important in music,

866
00:37:06,560 --> 00:37:09,310
requires a good
position representation.

867
00:37:09,310 --> 00:37:11,060
We actually found
significant improvements

868
00:37:11,060 --> 00:37:13,160
in the music transformer.

869
00:37:13,160 --> 00:37:15,570
Is it possible to play this?

870
00:37:15,570 --> 00:37:16,470
OK.

871
00:37:16,470 --> 00:37:18,920
So here is a--

872
00:37:18,920 --> 00:37:19,420
all right.

873
00:37:19,420 --> 00:37:20,740
Here's a priming sequence.

874
00:37:20,740 --> 00:37:22,900
This is work by--

875
00:37:22,900 --> 00:37:26,470
work by Anna Huang, by the way.

876
00:37:26,470 --> 00:37:29,462
[MUSIC PLAYING]

877
00:37:29,462 --> 00:37:32,270

878
00:37:32,270 --> 00:37:35,708
This is in-context
learning in music

879
00:37:35,708 --> 00:37:37,250
because you actually
see this prompt.

880
00:37:37,250 --> 00:37:40,575
And you ask the
model to complete it.

881
00:37:40,575 --> 00:37:42,200
So now this is the
vanilla transformer.

882
00:37:42,200 --> 00:37:45,215
And you can already--

883
00:37:45,215 --> 00:37:48,005
[MUSIC PLAYING]

884
00:37:48,005 --> 00:37:50,340

885
00:37:50,340 --> 00:37:53,370
So you can see that
these were using--

886
00:37:53,370 --> 00:37:54,870
we tried both learned
and sinusoids.

887
00:37:54,870 --> 00:37:57,750
And you can see that it
starts all peppy and happy,

888
00:37:57,750 --> 00:37:59,430
but then just languishes
into something

889
00:37:59,430 --> 00:38:01,710
really sad and confused.

890
00:38:01,710 --> 00:38:03,690
So it's not able
to capture these

891
00:38:03,690 --> 00:38:06,400
because music has these
interesting motifs, where--

892
00:38:06,400 --> 00:38:09,810
well, there's motifs
at different levels

893
00:38:09,810 --> 00:38:11,970
because there's some
repetition locally.

894
00:38:11,970 --> 00:38:14,590
But there's a repetition across
the entire piece as well.

895
00:38:14,590 --> 00:38:20,390
So now here this is with
the relative transformer.

896
00:38:20,390 --> 00:38:23,120
And this is with the
first approach, where

897
00:38:23,120 --> 00:38:24,620
we had relative embeddings.

898
00:38:24,620 --> 00:38:30,560
And we had to develop a
compute-efficient approach

899
00:38:30,560 --> 00:38:31,640
to actually--

900
00:38:31,640 --> 00:38:35,420
by using some matrix
calisthenics to actually put

901
00:38:35,420 --> 00:38:36,800
the logits in the right place.

902
00:38:36,800 --> 00:38:37,883
So you can read the paper.

903
00:38:37,883 --> 00:38:39,090
It's fun.

904
00:38:39,090 --> 00:38:40,730
So here is same prime sequence.

905
00:38:40,730 --> 00:38:44,170
And let's see the completion.

906
00:38:44,170 --> 00:38:46,746
Here.

907
00:38:46,746 --> 00:38:49,710
[MUSIC PLAYING]

908
00:38:49,710 --> 00:39:03,848

909
00:39:03,848 --> 00:39:06,140
So Anna, who's the first
author of this paper, and also

910
00:39:06,140 --> 00:39:08,140
a musician, tells me that
this actually captures

911
00:39:08,140 --> 00:39:10,100
a lot of structure in music.

912
00:39:10,100 --> 00:39:11,780
It sounds nicer than
the previous one.

913
00:39:11,780 --> 00:39:14,570
But maybe it depends on
what people's tastes are.

914
00:39:14,570 --> 00:39:17,450
Maybe some avant
garde jazz fan would

915
00:39:17,450 --> 00:39:20,300
like the second-- would
like the first piece.

916
00:39:20,300 --> 00:39:23,240
But the point here is
that the difference

917
00:39:23,240 --> 00:39:25,340
is pretty clear between
not working and working.

918
00:39:25,340 --> 00:39:27,800
And I think people-- it'd
be fun to try this out

919
00:39:27,800 --> 00:39:31,820
with the new rotary
position encodings.

920
00:39:31,820 --> 00:39:34,340
All right.

921
00:39:34,340 --> 00:39:38,510
So walking up-- now that we have
a-- we have a good mechanism--

922
00:39:38,510 --> 00:39:40,970
a better mechanism
than we originally

923
00:39:40,970 --> 00:39:43,460
had for modeling
relative distances.

924
00:39:43,460 --> 00:39:47,870
And there's advancements on top
of the rotary position encodings

925
00:39:47,870 --> 00:39:51,290
where, by adjusting the
base frequencies, you can--

926
00:39:51,290 --> 00:39:52,912
when you encounter
longer sequences,

927
00:39:52,912 --> 00:39:54,620
you can just adjust
the base frequencies.

928
00:39:54,620 --> 00:39:57,283
And then the model
is not going to--

929
00:39:57,283 --> 00:39:58,700
the model is not
going to degrade.

930
00:39:58,700 --> 00:40:01,010
So that has good properties.

931
00:40:01,010 --> 00:40:03,020
Probably the--
there's been-- there

932
00:40:03,020 --> 00:40:06,990
have been several
important contributions

933
00:40:06,990 --> 00:40:10,470
to the attention
piece itself, which

934
00:40:10,470 --> 00:40:12,360
is the primary workhorse here.

935
00:40:12,360 --> 00:40:16,230
It's the one that-- you can
think of it as-- it's either--

936
00:40:16,230 --> 00:40:18,480
there's induction heads that
are learning how to copy.

937
00:40:18,480 --> 00:40:21,390
Or maybe all it's really doing
is just routing information

938
00:40:21,390 --> 00:40:23,700
so that the giant feed
forward layers can actually

939
00:40:23,700 --> 00:40:25,320
learn the important features.

940
00:40:25,320 --> 00:40:28,200
But there's broadly two
classes of problems.

941
00:40:28,200 --> 00:40:31,018
There are two classes of issues
with the attention mechanism.

942
00:40:31,018 --> 00:40:33,060
One that was brought up
today that's very evident

943
00:40:33,060 --> 00:40:35,370
is long context itself.

944
00:40:35,370 --> 00:40:38,790
So the complexity,
as we remember,

945
00:40:38,790 --> 00:40:41,430
was quadratic in the
length of the sequence.

946
00:40:41,430 --> 00:40:43,710
And once your sequences
get very, very long--

947
00:40:43,710 --> 00:40:49,400
once your sequences get very,
very long, then it not only--

948
00:40:49,400 --> 00:40:50,280
There's one problem.

949
00:40:50,280 --> 00:40:53,230
It's going to become very-- it's
going to become computationally

950
00:40:53,230 --> 00:40:53,730
expensive.

951
00:40:53,730 --> 00:40:56,550
But it's also the logits that
are going to become infeasible.

952
00:40:56,550 --> 00:41:00,210
So there's generally a
few groups of papers.

953
00:41:00,210 --> 00:41:02,280
One is restricting
attention windows.

954
00:41:02,280 --> 00:41:08,760
And we did this for images where
we had local 1D and 2D attention

955
00:41:08,760 --> 00:41:09,600
for images.

956
00:41:09,600 --> 00:41:12,690
In the first one, we actually
just rasterized the image.

957
00:41:12,690 --> 00:41:14,970
And we had local 1D attention,
which is very similar

958
00:41:14,970 --> 00:41:18,330
to the sliding window
attention in the recent--

959
00:41:18,330 --> 00:41:19,830
in the [INAUDIBLE] paper.

960
00:41:19,830 --> 00:41:20,850
And then in the--

961
00:41:20,850 --> 00:41:24,090

962
00:41:24,090 --> 00:41:27,427
in the 2D case, we have
a spatial 2D attention.

963
00:41:27,427 --> 00:41:29,010
Then there were these
sparse versions,

964
00:41:29,010 --> 00:41:32,460
where you actually-- you
had these specific patterns

965
00:41:32,460 --> 00:41:34,180
over many layers.

966
00:41:34,180 --> 00:41:35,580
You could think about it as--

967
00:41:35,580 --> 00:41:39,025
if you have these sparse
matrices, how many of them

968
00:41:39,025 --> 00:41:40,650
do you have to multiply
with each other

969
00:41:40,650 --> 00:41:42,600
until you get a
really dense matrix?

970
00:41:42,600 --> 00:41:45,270
So roughly this turns
out to be-- so here,

971
00:41:45,270 --> 00:41:48,336
you can get connectivity.

972
00:41:48,336 --> 00:41:50,690
Is that for me?

973
00:41:50,690 --> 00:41:51,190
OK.

974
00:41:51,190 --> 00:41:55,300

975
00:41:55,300 --> 00:41:58,150
You can get connectivity
between distant pixels,

976
00:41:58,150 --> 00:42:03,940
or distant notes in a musical
tune, or words pretty quickly.

977
00:42:03,940 --> 00:42:05,920
And then there's the
second one, which

978
00:42:05,920 --> 00:42:08,290
is-- which there hasn't
been enough work.

979
00:42:08,290 --> 00:42:10,090
And there's some
challenges there.

980
00:42:10,090 --> 00:42:13,240
But it's these unstructured
sparse attention approaches.

981
00:42:13,240 --> 00:42:16,450
And they're typically-- they're
essentially-- what they're--

982
00:42:16,450 --> 00:42:19,960
at a higher level, what they're
really trying to do is--

983
00:42:19,960 --> 00:42:23,200
Imagine that I walked up to
you, and I told you that-- hey,

984
00:42:23,200 --> 00:42:28,840
these are the bunches
of tokens that just

985
00:42:28,840 --> 00:42:31,295
have very high intersimilarity.

986
00:42:31,295 --> 00:42:32,920
They're likely to
attend to each other.

987
00:42:32,920 --> 00:42:35,253
How quickly can I approximate
it without actually having

988
00:42:35,253 --> 00:42:37,180
to do the whole computation?

989
00:42:37,180 --> 00:42:38,770
Two approaches.

990
00:42:38,770 --> 00:42:40,930
In routing attention, you
use vector quantization.

991
00:42:40,930 --> 00:42:44,337
And in the LSH or the--

992
00:42:44,337 --> 00:42:45,420
I forget what it's called.

993
00:42:45,420 --> 00:42:47,100
I think I forgot the
name of the paper.

994
00:42:47,100 --> 00:42:51,060
But they used-- but in
this paper, they used LSH.

995
00:42:51,060 --> 00:42:57,840
And in the routing transformer,
most layers were actually local.

996
00:42:57,840 --> 00:42:59,910
The final layers,
which typically

997
00:42:59,910 --> 00:43:01,998
are the ones that like
to end up-- that end up

998
00:43:01,998 --> 00:43:03,540
doing modeling--
that end up modeling

999
00:43:03,540 --> 00:43:04,957
these long-distance
relationships,

1000
00:43:04,957 --> 00:43:08,310
were the ones that actually used
this content-based, unstructured

1001
00:43:08,310 --> 00:43:09,840
sparse attention.

1002
00:43:09,840 --> 00:43:12,270
And the results were-- the
results were generally better.

1003
00:43:12,270 --> 00:43:15,210
And it's also interesting
that maybe you

1004
00:43:15,210 --> 00:43:18,762
can build-- we can build models
with-- on very long sequences,

1005
00:43:18,762 --> 00:43:20,220
where most layers
are fairly local.

1006
00:43:20,220 --> 00:43:21,780
And you have only a few
layers that are actually doing

1007
00:43:21,780 --> 00:43:23,130
these long-distance attentions.

1008
00:43:23,130 --> 00:43:26,010
Now, one of the bigger
challenges there-- actually,

1009
00:43:26,010 --> 00:43:28,170
even though it ended up being--

1010
00:43:28,170 --> 00:43:30,120
even though you end up--

1011
00:43:30,120 --> 00:43:32,100
you end up nullifying
a lot of the FLOPs

1012
00:43:32,100 --> 00:43:34,050
that you would do if
you did full attention.

1013
00:43:34,050 --> 00:43:36,750
The problem always ends
up being memory movement.

1014
00:43:36,750 --> 00:43:38,550
Always ends up being
memory movement.

1015
00:43:38,550 --> 00:43:41,123
And there's still-- there's
still more innovation

1016
00:43:41,123 --> 00:43:41,790
to be done here.

1017
00:43:41,790 --> 00:43:43,650
Also, with memory
bandwidth improving,

1018
00:43:43,650 --> 00:43:46,660
maybe some of these approaches
become more feasible today

1019
00:43:46,660 --> 00:43:48,640
than they were
late-- than we did--

1020
00:43:48,640 --> 00:43:49,780
when we wrote these papers.

1021
00:43:49,780 --> 00:43:51,280
But this is an
interesting approach,

1022
00:43:51,280 --> 00:43:53,197
where you're essentially
trying to approximate

1023
00:43:53,197 --> 00:43:54,460
the original attention matrix.

1024
00:43:54,460 --> 00:43:54,960
Sorry.

1025
00:43:54,960 --> 00:43:55,930
This is a silly thing.

1026
00:43:55,930 --> 00:43:59,200
But clarification-- how
is this [INAUDIBLE] scheme

1027
00:43:59,200 --> 00:44:02,230
very different from just
convolutions that are sparse?

1028
00:44:02,230 --> 00:44:04,270
In the sense that
you're losing a lot

1029
00:44:04,270 --> 00:44:06,400
of the long-distance
or unrelated contexts

1030
00:44:06,400 --> 00:44:08,980
from any arbitrary
pairs of elements.

1031
00:44:08,980 --> 00:44:10,360
So how do you--

1032
00:44:10,360 --> 00:44:13,300
So I would say that this is
similar to the convolution

1033
00:44:13,300 --> 00:44:14,050
there.

1034
00:44:14,050 --> 00:44:19,470
If you did this perfectly,
then what you didn't attend to

1035
00:44:19,470 --> 00:44:21,910
would very likely have a
very low attention weight.

1036
00:44:21,910 --> 00:44:24,640

1037
00:44:24,640 --> 00:44:27,130
You're essentially trying to
guess, as best as you can,

1038
00:44:27,130 --> 00:44:29,620
what would have
attended to each other.

1039
00:44:29,620 --> 00:44:34,660
And so it uses its content
based on structure sparsity.

1040
00:44:34,660 --> 00:44:37,252
And there's probably
more interesting work

1041
00:44:37,252 --> 00:44:37,960
to be done there.

1042
00:44:37,960 --> 00:44:40,212
Maybe, instead of actually
just doing it a token

1043
00:44:40,212 --> 00:44:42,670
at a time, where you end up
doing a lot of memory movement,

1044
00:44:42,670 --> 00:44:45,520
you end up deciding which chunks
want to self-attend to which

1045
00:44:45,520 --> 00:44:46,020
chunks.

1046
00:44:46,020 --> 00:44:48,220
So then you just move
entire chunks at a time.

1047
00:44:48,220 --> 00:44:50,553
So there's-- I think there's
some interesting directions

1048
00:44:50,553 --> 00:44:51,370
here.

1049
00:44:51,370 --> 00:44:52,840
And of course--

1050
00:44:52,840 --> 00:44:57,340
And frankly, the ones
that ended up sticking

1051
00:44:57,340 --> 00:45:00,070
are the simplest
ones that are also--

1052
00:45:00,070 --> 00:45:02,910
that are-- because
structured sparsity is easy.

1053
00:45:02,910 --> 00:45:07,060
You're able to optimize it
easily on modern accelerators.

1054
00:45:07,060 --> 00:45:10,960
So again, physics-- you should
make physics your friend.

1055
00:45:10,960 --> 00:45:12,230
Yeah.

1056
00:45:12,230 --> 00:45:14,140
And so, typically,
local attention,

1057
00:45:14,140 --> 00:45:16,210
or sliding into attention--

1058
00:45:16,210 --> 00:45:18,910
we're still seeing it
often appear and do well.

1059
00:45:18,910 --> 00:45:22,480
These other very--
really wild and--

1060
00:45:22,480 --> 00:45:24,790
but very expressive,
unstructured, sparse attention

1061
00:45:24,790 --> 00:45:27,445
approaches typically
haven't quite succeeded.

1062
00:45:27,445 --> 00:45:29,320
There's, of course,
linear attention variants

1063
00:45:29,320 --> 00:45:32,740
that I don't think today are
in any of the state of the art

1064
00:45:32,740 --> 00:45:33,610
architectures.

1065
00:45:33,610 --> 00:45:36,068
There were other approaches
that-- hey, instead of actually

1066
00:45:36,068 --> 00:45:38,200
doing n squared,
you do n squared

1067
00:45:38,200 --> 00:45:40,750
d, where you do
n-- you have some--

1068
00:45:40,750 --> 00:45:44,980
you learn new k embeddings,
where you do NKD,

1069
00:45:44,980 --> 00:45:46,510
and then you do NDK.

1070
00:45:46,510 --> 00:45:49,830
So you basically factor
it just like an analog

1071
00:45:49,830 --> 00:45:51,000
of matrix factorization.

1072
00:45:51,000 --> 00:45:53,100
Something that's one
other approach that's

1073
00:45:53,100 --> 00:45:56,580
interesting, that I would like
myself to actually investigate,

1074
00:45:56,580 --> 00:45:57,330
is--

1075
00:45:57,330 --> 00:46:00,060
we are seeing, in general,
using retrieval as a tool.

1076
00:46:00,060 --> 00:46:03,780
So why don't you just pretend
that your memories themselves

1077
00:46:03,780 --> 00:46:05,970
were documents and use
retrieval as a tool there?

1078
00:46:05,970 --> 00:46:09,360
So the memorizing
transformer basically--

1079
00:46:09,360 --> 00:46:11,490
it essentially does
a mix of local.

1080
00:46:11,490 --> 00:46:14,930
And it then retrieves from
very, very long memories.

1081
00:46:14,930 --> 00:46:16,680
And they find that you
don't need to train

1082
00:46:16,680 --> 00:46:17,760
the model from scratch.

1083
00:46:17,760 --> 00:46:20,760
All you need to do is
adapt with this approach

1084
00:46:20,760 --> 00:46:23,402
on some small amount
of data, and you're

1085
00:46:23,402 --> 00:46:25,110
able to learn a good
retrieval mechanism.

1086
00:46:25,110 --> 00:46:27,270
I think it's quite interesting.

1087
00:46:27,270 --> 00:46:32,310
It still comes in this
content-based decision

1088
00:46:32,310 --> 00:46:33,480
of what I should attend to.

1089
00:46:33,480 --> 00:46:36,450
But I like the fact that
it just makes retrieval

1090
00:46:36,450 --> 00:46:38,820
a tool that you can use
either on your own memories.

1091
00:46:38,820 --> 00:46:40,620
Or you could use
it on documents.

1092
00:46:40,620 --> 00:46:42,480
It's a nice generalizing.

1093
00:46:42,480 --> 00:46:45,370
It's a nice general view
of looking at things.

1094
00:46:45,370 --> 00:46:49,180
So now the second piece,
which you basically--

1095
00:46:49,180 --> 00:46:51,123
you run into not all--

1096
00:46:51,123 --> 00:46:53,290
you run into the issue that
not all FLOPs are equal.

1097
00:46:53,290 --> 00:46:58,540
So if you look at the-- if you
look at the memory hierarchy,

1098
00:46:58,540 --> 00:47:00,850
a lot of your activations in--

1099
00:47:00,850 --> 00:47:04,390
that are stored in the GPU
HBM, which today, in the H100s,

1100
00:47:04,390 --> 00:47:11,710
is about 80 gigabytes-- but
the H100 is 80 gigabytes.

1101
00:47:11,710 --> 00:47:13,870
And the A100s used
to be 40 gigabytes.

1102
00:47:13,870 --> 00:47:16,630
So there's limited amount
of high-bandwidth memory.

1103
00:47:16,630 --> 00:47:19,420
And so you have to first go
from high-bandwidth memory

1104
00:47:19,420 --> 00:47:20,455
to the SRAM.

1105
00:47:20,455 --> 00:47:23,080
And then you have to go to the
compute elements, and then back.

1106
00:47:23,080 --> 00:47:25,690
So every single
time-- and this is--

1107
00:47:25,690 --> 00:47:31,270
it probably-- and whenever--

1108
00:47:31,270 --> 00:47:33,970
If interested, you can
look at roofline analysis.

1109
00:47:33,970 --> 00:47:39,160
The roofline analysis actually
gives you a nice picture

1110
00:47:39,160 --> 00:47:44,670
to characterize for any device
where you would need-- where

1111
00:47:44,670 --> 00:47:48,272
your workload or
operation needs to be so

1112
00:47:48,272 --> 00:47:49,980
that you can actually
effectively utilize

1113
00:47:49,980 --> 00:47:50,910
the compute as much.

1114
00:47:50,910 --> 00:47:53,160
You want to be compute-bound
because, ultimately,

1115
00:47:53,160 --> 00:47:54,840
if you don't calculate
representations--

1116
00:47:54,840 --> 00:47:57,090
if you don't calculate, you're
not going to get any output.

1117
00:47:57,090 --> 00:47:59,215
But if you spend a lot of
time moving things around

1118
00:47:59,215 --> 00:48:02,430
and spend less relative
time calculating,

1119
00:48:02,430 --> 00:48:06,930
then you're actually--
you're wasting effort.

1120
00:48:06,930 --> 00:48:09,720
So one of the-- so [INAUDIBLE]
standard attention mechanism.

1121
00:48:09,720 --> 00:48:11,215
One of the issues is that--

1122
00:48:11,215 --> 00:48:12,840
so imagine you have
your queries, keys,

1123
00:48:12,840 --> 00:48:15,000
and values all in your memory.

1124
00:48:15,000 --> 00:48:17,100
So then you need to then--

1125
00:48:17,100 --> 00:48:19,530
your standard approach would
be, you move it to-- you

1126
00:48:19,530 --> 00:48:20,610
move it from HBM.

1127
00:48:20,610 --> 00:48:21,807
You do the calculations.

1128
00:48:21,807 --> 00:48:22,890
You compute the attention.

1129
00:48:22,890 --> 00:48:24,150
You compute the logits.

1130
00:48:24,150 --> 00:48:26,550
You move logits back into HBM.

1131
00:48:26,550 --> 00:48:28,440
And then you compute softmax--

1132
00:48:28,440 --> 00:48:30,570
the softmax back into HBM.

1133
00:48:30,570 --> 00:48:35,550
And then you basically load the
probabilities and the values

1134
00:48:35,550 --> 00:48:37,980
then to then finally
compute the outputs.

1135
00:48:37,980 --> 00:48:41,520
So the arithmetic
intensity, or--

1136
00:48:41,520 --> 00:48:43,930
the arithmetic intensity
or operational intensity,

1137
00:48:43,930 --> 00:48:46,060
which is the amount
of FLOPs that you

1138
00:48:46,060 --> 00:48:48,760
do per byte on attention--
even though it's

1139
00:48:48,760 --> 00:48:51,370
less FLOPs than, say,
a 1 by 1 convolution,

1140
00:48:51,370 --> 00:48:53,800
it had more-- it is lower
because it typically

1141
00:48:53,800 --> 00:48:56,437
has more memory movement,
whereas 1 by 1 convolutions

1142
00:48:56,437 --> 00:48:57,520
have less memory movement.

1143
00:48:57,520 --> 00:48:58,540
You just move the weights.

1144
00:48:58,540 --> 00:48:59,440
You move the activations.

1145
00:48:59,440 --> 00:49:00,220
You do the calculations.

1146
00:49:00,220 --> 00:49:01,300
Then you bring them back.

1147
00:49:01,300 --> 00:49:02,800
And same goes for
convolutions, too.

1148
00:49:02,800 --> 00:49:04,900
And convolutions have a very
high arithmetic intensity.

1149
00:49:04,900 --> 00:49:07,090
So it's not that you just want
the highest arithmetic intensity

1150
00:49:07,090 --> 00:49:08,650
or operational
intensity operations

1151
00:49:08,650 --> 00:49:11,560
because you still want to
have useful parameters.

1152
00:49:11,560 --> 00:49:13,600
So it's a trade-off.

1153
00:49:13,600 --> 00:49:18,227
So a lot of-- so there's a bunch
of improvements that will stick.

1154
00:49:18,227 --> 00:49:19,810
It's almost-- they're
almost certain--

1155
00:49:19,810 --> 00:49:22,840
likely to stay that try
to combat this issue,

1156
00:49:22,840 --> 00:49:25,600
both at training time, because
your logits can get really big,

1157
00:49:25,600 --> 00:49:28,080
but also at inference
time, when your KD--

1158
00:49:28,080 --> 00:49:30,580
when you're doing attention and
when you're doing inference,

1159
00:49:30,580 --> 00:49:33,010
then you have a single query,
but your KD cache, right?

1160
00:49:33,010 --> 00:49:35,112
You have to maintain
your keys and values.

1161
00:49:35,112 --> 00:49:37,570
That can grow quite a bit, so
you have to move that around.

1162
00:49:37,570 --> 00:49:40,540
And so the first step is simple.

1163
00:49:40,540 --> 00:49:43,750
Let's just decrease
the activation memory.

1164
00:49:43,750 --> 00:49:46,850
So the multi-query approach,
where it's basically

1165
00:49:46,850 --> 00:49:48,940
a multiple-- so you reduce--

1166
00:49:48,940 --> 00:49:52,270
you have multiple queries,
but you reduce the number

1167
00:49:52,270 --> 00:49:54,185
of read heads to just one.

1168
00:49:54,185 --> 00:49:55,810
So you have just one
key and one value.

1169
00:49:55,810 --> 00:49:57,730
That does reduce
your expressivity.

1170
00:49:57,730 --> 00:50:01,420
So grouped-query-- which is now
a simple balance that basically

1171
00:50:01,420 --> 00:50:03,250
says, hey, let's
not take the extreme

1172
00:50:03,250 --> 00:50:06,250
of having all this
temporary activation memory.

1173
00:50:06,250 --> 00:50:07,870
Let's actually group
it so different

1174
00:50:07,870 --> 00:50:11,320
queries-- so a bunch of queries
will attend to the same keys

1175
00:50:11,320 --> 00:50:12,370
and values.

1176
00:50:12,370 --> 00:50:16,270
And then what ends
up happening is--

1177
00:50:16,270 --> 00:50:18,220
another point to note
here is that all of this

1178
00:50:18,220 --> 00:50:21,490
is relative because most of
the work in these very, very--

1179
00:50:21,490 --> 00:50:23,200
Oh, by the way, third
approach, actually,

1180
00:50:23,200 --> 00:50:26,830
that I should say
of not worrying

1181
00:50:26,830 --> 00:50:29,503
about your attention is just
to make your model really big.

1182
00:50:29,503 --> 00:50:32,170
Because then you just care about
your feed forward computations.

1183
00:50:32,170 --> 00:50:34,795
And your attention computations
are just a small slice of that,

1184
00:50:34,795 --> 00:50:36,040
so you don't worry about it.

1185
00:50:36,040 --> 00:50:38,110
So typically, in
these larger models--

1186
00:50:38,110 --> 00:50:42,430
even though grouped query
attention has more activation

1187
00:50:42,430 --> 00:50:44,920
memory than multi-query
with these large models,

1188
00:50:44,920 --> 00:50:46,690
it's still a much larger--

1189
00:50:46,690 --> 00:50:48,070
it's not a much larger--

1190
00:50:48,070 --> 00:50:50,140
it's still a smaller proportion
of what you're doing in the feed

1191
00:50:50,140 --> 00:50:50,640
forward.

1192
00:50:50,640 --> 00:50:51,730
So you're certifying.

1193
00:50:51,730 --> 00:50:53,200
So, I guess, three things.

1194
00:50:53,200 --> 00:50:53,800
Ignore.

1195
00:50:53,800 --> 00:50:54,790
Make it really big.

1196
00:50:54,790 --> 00:50:56,110
Make it really big.

1197
00:50:56,110 --> 00:50:58,720
Second is, I guess, you--

1198
00:50:58,720 --> 00:51:02,020
by even the-- with
prolonged context,

1199
00:51:02,020 --> 00:51:03,313
you can do some of the--

1200
00:51:03,313 --> 00:51:05,230
some of these approaches
that we talked about.

1201
00:51:05,230 --> 00:51:08,020
But then you also have
these system optimizations,

1202
00:51:08,020 --> 00:51:10,520
which are pretty cool.

1203
00:51:10,520 --> 00:51:14,170
So the softmax has an
interesting property that you

1204
00:51:14,170 --> 00:51:15,520
can compute in online fashion.

1205
00:51:15,520 --> 00:51:17,830
You can compute
it incrementally.

1206
00:51:17,830 --> 00:51:19,330
If you've got a
bunch of logits-- so

1207
00:51:19,330 --> 00:51:21,035
you're streaming them.

1208
00:51:21,035 --> 00:51:23,410
If you've got a partial softmax
and a new logit comes in,

1209
00:51:23,410 --> 00:51:24,340
you can update it.

1210
00:51:24,340 --> 00:51:26,320
You can update it in
an online fashion.

1211
00:51:26,320 --> 00:51:27,700
So what does that mean?

1212
00:51:27,700 --> 00:51:31,990
That means that now you
never need it to write

1213
00:51:31,990 --> 00:51:34,390
logits or the P's into the HBM.

1214
00:51:34,390 --> 00:51:35,537
So you save a lot.

1215
00:51:35,537 --> 00:51:37,120
If there's an extremely
long sequence,

1216
00:51:37,120 --> 00:51:38,560
you end up writing a lot.

1217
00:51:38,560 --> 00:51:40,090
So you save on that.

1218
00:51:40,090 --> 00:51:43,270
And both these approaches
end up-- in one case,

1219
00:51:43,270 --> 00:51:47,720
the first paper was on TPUs
that introduced this property,

1220
00:51:47,720 --> 00:51:50,883
or took advantage of this
property, [INAUDIBLE]

1221
00:51:50,883 --> 00:51:52,300
the property to
be able to compute

1222
00:51:52,300 --> 00:51:53,890
the softmax in an
online fashion,

1223
00:51:53,890 --> 00:51:58,120
and the second paper, which
is now flash attention today.

1224
00:51:58,120 --> 00:51:59,890
They've had many advancements.

1225
00:51:59,890 --> 00:52:03,370
They actually had some systems
level optimization, where they--

1226
00:52:03,370 --> 00:52:07,360
now you can actually have very,
very long sequences on GPUs--

1227
00:52:07,360 --> 00:52:11,950
the optimizations of GPUs
by basically not moving

1228
00:52:11,950 --> 00:52:14,140
the logits back into
HBM using this online--

1229
00:52:14,140 --> 00:52:16,450
using this property, and
also writing the right forms

1230
00:52:16,450 --> 00:52:19,960
that use [INAUDIBLE] and
everything, use the GPU.

1231
00:52:19,960 --> 00:52:22,105
Any questions?

1232
00:52:22,105 --> 00:52:23,403
OK.

1233
00:52:23,403 --> 00:52:24,070
What's the time?

1234
00:52:24,070 --> 00:52:27,350

1235
00:52:27,350 --> 00:52:28,780
So we are basically--

1236
00:52:28,780 --> 00:52:29,470
20 minutes.

1237
00:52:29,470 --> 00:52:31,270
So I'll finish in time.

1238
00:52:31,270 --> 00:52:33,010
So I just covered these two.

1239
00:52:33,010 --> 00:52:34,810
There's many, many.

1240
00:52:34,810 --> 00:52:40,180
I guess there's other
important improvements.

1241
00:52:40,180 --> 00:52:44,110
So we talked about the pre and
post versus post-LayerNorm.

1242
00:52:44,110 --> 00:52:47,680
There's been some changes of the
feed forward layers themselves.

1243
00:52:47,680 --> 00:52:50,278
You can stare at the
feed forward layers.

1244
00:52:50,278 --> 00:52:51,820
If you stare at
anything long enough,

1245
00:52:51,820 --> 00:52:52,780
everything becomes attention.

1246
00:52:52,780 --> 00:52:54,363
But it's true in the
feed forward case

1247
00:52:54,363 --> 00:52:57,460
that, if you look at it, you
can think about them as--

1248
00:52:57,460 --> 00:52:58,480
it looks like attention.

1249
00:52:58,480 --> 00:53:03,732
And there was a paper that
turned that into a bit--

1250
00:53:03,732 --> 00:53:05,440
turned that-- turned
those into memories.

1251
00:53:05,440 --> 00:53:06,640
It was originally by Facebook.

1252
00:53:06,640 --> 00:53:07,780
I actually forget what it was.

1253
00:53:07,780 --> 00:53:08,500
But it didn't--

1254
00:53:08,500 --> 00:53:11,860
And the feed forward
layers just stayed.

1255
00:53:11,860 --> 00:53:15,880
We typically haven't seen a
lot of improvements on them.

1256
00:53:15,880 --> 00:53:17,910
There have been some--

1257
00:53:17,910 --> 00:53:20,410
there have been some efforts
on higher order attention right

1258
00:53:20,410 --> 00:53:20,920
now.

1259
00:53:20,920 --> 00:53:22,060
Attention, if you
think about it,

1260
00:53:22,060 --> 00:53:23,290
is a third-order interaction.

1261
00:53:23,290 --> 00:53:25,780
You have queries,
keys, and values, but--

1262
00:53:25,780 --> 00:53:26,450
right now.

1263
00:53:26,450 --> 00:53:29,690
But you could imagine actually
having four-order interactions,

1264
00:53:29,690 --> 00:53:32,480
where you're actually computing
logits of pairs of things

1265
00:53:32,480 --> 00:53:33,765
against all pairs of things.

1266
00:53:33,765 --> 00:53:36,140
So these are now higher order
interactions, where now you

1267
00:53:36,140 --> 00:53:39,920
can have complicated geometries
that you actually include

1268
00:53:39,920 --> 00:53:42,840
in your attention computation.

1269
00:53:42,840 --> 00:53:44,840
And maybe it's important
for, say, biology, or--

